window.SUCHE=[{"t":"Lesson 1.1: Taxi or private hire (Mietwagen)?","u":"lektion-1-1-taxi-oder-mietwagen.html","h":["When does the Passenger Transport Act apply?","When does the PBefG not apply?","Scheduled and occasional transport","What taxi and Mietwagen have in common","The differences at a glance","The duty to return for Mietwagen","Exceptions and special rules","What does this mean for Uber, Bolt and co.?","Typical mistakes","Summary"],"x":"Taxis and private hire cars ( Mietwagen ) look similar on the street. Legally, they are two different types of transport with their own rules. The exam almost always has a question on this. Many people mix up the duty to return ( Rückkehrpflicht ) and the obligation to carry ( Beförderungspflicht ). If you drive for Uber or Bolt, you almost always drive a Mietwagen. When does the Passenger Transport Act apply? The Passenger Transport Act ( Personenbeförderungsgesetz, PBefG ) applies when someone carries people in a motor vehicle for payment or on a business basis ( entgeltlich oder geschäftsmäßig ). Payment does not only mean money. Other economic benefits also count. Anyone covered by the PBefG needs a licence ( Genehmigung ) from the authority. Without a licence the trip is forbidden. § 2 (1) PBefG (simplified) Anyone who carries passengers as described in § 1 must have a licence. They are then an “operator” ( Unternehmer ) under the Act. When does the PBefG not apply? For trips by car there are two important exceptions § 1 (2) No. 1 PBefG : The trip is free of charge ( unentgeltlich ). The total payment is not higher than the amount in the Federal Travel Expenses Act, currently 30 cents per kilometre § 5 (2) BRKG . Example: a ride share where everyone only shares the fuel costs. Another exception applies to ambulances if patients need medical care during the trip § 1 (2) No. 2 PBefG . Scheduled and occasional transport The PBefG distinguishes two big groups. Scheduled transport ( Linienverkehr ) runs on a fixed route with a timetable, like a city bus. Everything else is occasional transport ( Gelegenheitsverkehr ). The law allows only four forms of it § 46 (2) PBefG : Taxis Verkehr mit Taxen § 47 PBefG Excursions and holiday trips by coach § 48 PBefG Hired coaches and private hire cars Mietomnibusse und Mietwagen § 49 PBefG Pooled on-demand transport gebündelter Bedarfsverkehr shared rides on request, “pooling” § 50 PBefG For this exam, the taxi and the Mietwagen matter most. You should know pooled on-demand transport so you can tell it apart. What taxi and Mietwagen have in common Licence required No trips for money without a licence from the authority. § 2 PBefG Four requirements The business is safe and financially sound. The operator is reliable and professionally competent. The registered office is in Germany. § 13 (1) PBefG Professional competence You usually prove it with exactly this exam ( Fachkundeprüfung ). It is the same for taxi and Mietwagen. § 13 PBefG, PBZugV Maximum 5 years The licence is valid for a maximum of five years. Then you apply again. § 16 (4) PBefG You will learn more about the licence in lesson 1.2 . The differences at a glance This table is the heart of the lesson. On a phone you can swipe it sideways. Topic Taxi Mietwagen (private hire) Where does the booking come from? At the taxi rank , hailed on the street, by radio or app, at the registered office. § 47 (1) PBefG Only via the registered office ( Betriebssitz ) or the operator's home. This also applies to app bookings. § 49 (4) PBefG Waiting for customers At taxi ranks approved by the authority, in principle only in the municipality of the registered office. § 47 (2) PBefG Not on the street. After every trip the duty to return ( Rückkehrpflicht ) applies. § 49 (4) PBefG Must I take everyone? Yes. The obligation to carry ( Beförderungspflicht ) applies within the mandatory service area , plus the obligation to operate . §§ 21, 22, 47 (4) PBefG No. The obligations to operate and to carry do not apply to Mietwagen. § 49 (4) PBefG Price Fixed taxi tariff set by regulation. It is binding. For pre-booked trips, fixed prices or a price range are possible. § 51 PBefG In principle freely agreed . The authority may set minimum fares . § 51a (1) PBefG Appearance Light ivory ( RAL 1015 ) with an illuminated taxi roof sign . § 26 (1) BOKraft Must not be confused with a taxi. No signs reserved for taxis. § 49 (4) PBefG Meter Calibrated taximeter ( Fahrpreisanzeiger ) or an approved software system. § 28 BOKraft Calibrated odometer ( Wegstreckenzähler ) or an approved software system. § 30 BOKraft Licence The authority checks whether the local taxi trade still works. New applicants get their first licence for 2 years . § 13 (4) and (5) PBefG No such check. Records No special duty for bookings. Record every booking (on paper or electronically) and keep it for 1 year . § 49 (4) PBefG Outside advertising For both only on the side doors . No political or religious advertising on taxis. § 26 (2) BOKraft Details are set by the federal states and municipalities, for example in the local taxi regulations ( Taxenordnung ). Exceptions are possible there, for example regarding the colour. The duty to return for Mietwagen This is the most important rule for Mietwagen. It prevents private hire cars from waiting for customers on the street like taxis. After every trip there is only one question: Step 1 The passenger has arrived. The trip is over. → Question Was there a new booking before or during the trip that came in via the registered office ? → Yes The driver goes straight to the next passenger. No The driver returns to the registered office without delay ( unverzüglich ). Waiting at the roadside, at the station or at the airport is forbidden. § 49 (4) PBefG (simplified) Mietwagen may only carry out bookings received at the registered office or the operator's home. After the trip the Mietwagen returns to the registered office without delay, unless it received a new booking from its office before the trip or by phone during the trip. Exceptions and special rules New booking: If the booking came via the registered office before or during the trip, the driver may continue directly. Large rural municipalities: The authority may allow Mietwagen to wait at other approved parking locations ( Abstellorte ). The basis is a distance of at least 15 km between the registered office and the parking location, or between several locations. "},{"t":"Lesson 1.2: Licence, access to the profession and inspections","u":"lektion-1-2-genehmigung-berufszugang.html","h":["The four requirements","Reliability","Financial standing: how much equity?","Proof and its deadlines","Professional competence: three ways","The procedure: from application to licence","The licence document","Changes, transfer, death","If the operator dies or becomes unable to act","Inspections, fines and revocation","Fines","Revocation of the licence","Trips without a licence","Typical mistakes","Summary"],"x":"Without a licence ( Genehmigung ) you may not carry a single passenger for money. In this lesson you learn which requirements you must meet, how much equity you need, how the application works and what happens if you break the rules. Exam questions on equity and deadlines are very common. The four requirements The authority may only grant the licence if all four requirements are met § 13 (1) sentence 1 PBefG : 1. Safety and financial capacity Sicherheit und Leistungsfähigkeit The business is safe. You have enough money to run it properly (financial standing). No. 1 2. Reliability Zuverlässigkeit There are no facts showing that you or your managers are unreliable. No. 2 3. Professional competence fachliche Eignung You, or the person managing the business, are professionally competent. No. 3 4. Office in Germany Betriebssitz im Inland Your registered office or branch is in Germany. No. 4 How these points are checked is set out in the Regulation on Access to the Profession ( Berufszugangsverordnung für den Straßenpersonenverkehr, PBZugV ). Memory aid S afe, R eliable, C ompetent, G erman office: “ S mart R iders C an G o”. In German: Sicherheit, Zuverlässigkeit, fachliche Eignung, Inland. Reliability You are reliable if nothing suggests that you will run the business against the law or endanger the public. Signs of unreliability are in particular § 1 (1) PBZugV : final convictions for serious crimes, serious breaches of the PBefG and its regulations, serious breaches of employment and social law, for example working and rest times, serious breaches of traffic and vehicle safety rules (StVG, StVO, StVZO), serious breaches of tax and levy duties, driving without motor liability insurance (§ 6 Compulsory Insurance Act), serious breaches of environmental rules. As proof, the authority can ask for a certificate of good conduct ( Führungszeugnis ) and an extract from the central trade register , as well as certificates from the tax office and social insurance § 1 (3) PBZugV . Financial standing: how much equity? You must have enough money to start and run the business properly. For taxi and Mietwagen there is a fixed minimum: equity and reserves ( Eigenkapital und Reserven ) must be at least one quarter of the amounts EU law sets for bus companies § 2 (1) No. 2 PBZugV, Art. 7 Reg. (EC) 1071/2009 . Vehicle Bus (EU law) Taxi / Mietwagen (¼) first vehicle 9.000 € 2.250 € each additional vehicle 5.000 € 1.250 € Example You want to start with 3 Mietwagen. 1st vehicle = 2.250 € 2nd and 3rd vehicle = 2 × 1.250 € = 2.500 € Minimum equity = 4.750 € In addition, your ability to pay must not be at risk. You must not have significant arrears in taxes or social insurance contributions § 2 (1) No. 1 PBZugV . Proof and its deadlines Document Issued by May be at most … old Certificate of equity capital ( Eigenkapitalbescheinigung ) or confirmed statement of assets Tax adviser, auditor or bank 1 year Certificates of good standing ( Unbedenklichkeitsbescheinigungen ) Tax office, municipality, health insurers, Berufsgenossenschaft 3 months § 2 (2) PBZugV Reserves can also include, for example, unencumbered private assets of the liable operator or subordinated loans § 2 (3) PBZugV . Professional competence: three ways You are professionally competent if you have the knowledge listed in Annex 3 of the PBZugV § 3 (2) PBZugV . That is exactly the content of this course. You can prove it in three ways: The exam ( Fachkundeprüfung ) at the IHK where you live . This is the normal way. §§ 4, 5 PBZugV An equivalent final examination recognised by the state authority. § 6 PBZugV Management experience of at least 3 years in a German taxi or Mietwagen company. It must have ended no more than 2 years before the application. The IHK checks this and may hold an interview. § 7 PBZugV The owner does not have to be competent personally. It is enough if a person appointed to manage the business is, for example an employed operations manager § 13 (1) No. 3 PBefG . The procedure: from application to licence Apply to the licensing authority, usually the city or district administration, with proof of all four requirements and details of the vehicles. Consultation: the authority asks for opinions from the municipality of the office, the trade supervisory office, the IHK , trade unions and transport associations. They have 2 weeks . § 14 (2) and (4) PBefG Decision within 3 months . The authority can extend this once by up to 3 months . If it does not decide in time, the licence is deemed granted. § 15 (1) PBefG Licence document ( Genehmigungsurkunde ): you receive it once the decision is final. There is no licence “on trial” or subject to revocation. § 15 (2) and (4) PBefG Extra for taxis The authority checks whether new taxis would endanger the functioning of the local taxi trade . It looks at demand, taxi density, income and cost situation and business closures. For this it can set an observation period of at most 1 year . That is why many cities have waiting lists. § 13 (4) PBefG Applications are processed in order of receipt , separately for new applicants and existing operators. Anyone who does not intend to run the taxi business as their main occupation is placed further back. New applicants get their first licence for only 2 years and may not transfer it during that time. § 13 (5) PBefG The licence document The licence document states, among other things § 17 (1) PBefG : name, residence and registered office of the operator, the type of transport (taxi or Mietwagen), the period of validity, conditions and requirements, the registration numbers of the vehicles used. Everyday duties Carry it: every vehicle must carry the licence or a shortened official copy ( gekürzte amtliche Ausfertigung ), on paper or electronically. Show it on request. § 17 (4) PBefG Vehicle change: if you replace a vehicle or take one out of service, submit the document to the authority for amendment. § 17 (2) PBefG Changes, transfer, death You also need a licenc"},{"t":"Lesson 1.3: Fares and the obligation to carry","u":"lektion-1-3-tarife-befoerderungspflicht.html","h":["Who sets the taxi tariff?","What can the tariff contain?","The tariff obligation","The roof sign","Exceptions to the tariff obligation","1. Fixed price or price range for pre-booked trips","2. Special agreements","The mandatory service area","The obligation to carry","When may the driver refuse or exclude someone?","The obligation to operate","Prices for Mietwagen","Typical mistakes","Summary"],"x":"In a taxi you cannot set the price yourself. It is in the taxi tariff ( Taxitarif ), and in principle you must take everyone. Since 2021 there are exceptions: fixed prices and price ranges for pre-booked trips. Here you learn who sets the tariff, what it contains, when you may refuse a trip and what applies to Mietwagen. Who sets the taxi tariff? The PBefG allows the state government to set taxi fares by regulation ( Rechtsverordnung ). It can delegate this task § 51 (1) PBefG . In practice this is usually done by the districts and independent cities . The tariff is then published in a taxi tariff regulation, often together with the local taxi regulations ( Taxenordnung ). Before the tariff is set or changed, the IHK , trade unions and transport associations, among others, are consulted § 51 (3) with § 14 (2) PBefG . Several authorities can agree on a common tariff for a larger area § 51 (4) PBefG . What can the tariff contain? The regulation can cover, among other things § 51 (1) sentence 2 PBefG : Base fare Grundpreis A fixed amount for every trip. Price per km Kilometerpreis Price per kilometre driven, often in steps. Time price Zeitpreis For waiting time and slow driving, e.g. in traffic jams. Fixed prices Festpreise For certain routes, e.g. to the airport. Surcharges Zuschläge For example for a large taxi or a lot of luggage. Payment Advance payment, billing, means of payment (e.g. card payment). Sample tariff (sample values) Base fare 4,50 € 1st to 5th km 2,50 € per km from the 6th km 2,20 € per km Waiting time 36,00 € per hour Trip of 8 km with 5 minutes in traffic: Base fare = 4,50 € 5 km × 2,50 € = 12,50 € 3 km × 2,20 € = 6,60 € 5 min ÷ 60 × 36,00 € = 3,00 € Fare = 26,60 € (incl. 7 % VAT) Taxi tariffs are final prices including VAT . More in lesson 1.10 . More tasks in the calculation trainer . The tariff obligation The set tariff is binding . You may neither charge more nor less . Discounts are only allowed if they apply to everyone under the same conditions. Other discounts are forbidden and void § 51 (5) with § 39 (3) PBefG . Examples “10 % off for you as a regular customer.” Forbidden unless it is in the tariff. “At night it costs 5 € more because I'm tired.” Forbidden if the tariff has no night surcharge. Breaking the tariff obligation can cost a fine of up to 10.000 € . § 61 PBefG The taxi charges with the calibrated taximeter § 28 BOKraft . The driver takes the shortest route unless the passenger wants something else. He may take another route if it is better for traffic or cheaper and the passenger agrees § 38 BOKraft . The roof sign Within the tariff area the taxi roof sign must be lit when the taxi is free. At a taxi rank it does not have to be lit. During a booked trip it is switched off § 39 BOKraft . So people on the street can see at once whether a taxi is free. Exceptions to the tariff obligation 1. Fixed price or price range for pre-booked trips Since the PBefG reform of 2021, the tariff can provide for pre-booked trips § 51 (1) sentence 4 PBefG : fixed prices , or a price range with a minimum and maximum price (often called a “fare corridor”, Tarifkorridor ). Within the range, passenger and operator agree the price freely before the trip starts . This lets taxis offer a fixed price for app bookings, similar to Mietwagen. Whether and how this applies to you is set in your local tariff. 2. Special agreements With large customers, for example a company or a health insurer, the operator may agree a different price within the mandatory service area. But only if all four conditions are met § 51 (2) PBefG : There is a fixed period , a minimum number of trips or a minimum monthly turnover . The order of the transport market is not disturbed. Prices and conditions are agreed in writing . The regulation provides for approval or notification to the authority. The mandatory service area The mandatory service area ( Pflichtfahrbereich ) is the area where the taxi tariff applies, usually the municipality or the district. Only here do the tariff obligation and the obligation to carry apply § 47 (4) PBefG . If a passenger travels beyond the mandatory service area, local rules decide how the price for the part outside is calculated. Often it may be agreed freely. Then agree it before the trip starts , otherwise there will be arguments. If nothing is agreed, the tariff usually continues to apply. Aachen According to the City of Aachen taxi tariff of 2021, the mandatory service area covers the whole StädteRegion Aachen . Always check the current version with the city or the StädteRegion. The obligation to carry A taxi must take passengers. This is the obligation to carry ( Beförderungspflicht ). It applies if all three conditions are met § 22 PBefG : The passenger complies with the conditions of carriage . The trip is possible with the vehicles normally used . (A normal taxi does not have to carry 8 people or a sofa.) The trip is not prevented by circumstances the operator cannot avoid. (For example: the road is closed due to a storm.) For taxis it applies only in the mandatory service area . For Mietwagen it does not apply at all § 47 (4), § 49 (4) PBefG . Not a reason to refuse “The trip is too short.” “I'd rather have a long trip.” “My shift ends soon.” (as long as the taxi is on duty) The passenger's origin, skin colour, religion or disability When may the driver refuse or exclude someone? Situation Rule Facts suggest the passenger is a danger to safety, the operation or other passengers, e.g. very drunk and aggressive. May refuse § 13 BOKraft The passenger breaks the rules despite a warning , e.g. opens the door while driving or throws things out of the window. May exclude § 14 (4) BOKraft The passenger wants to take dangerous substances : explosive, highly flammable, radioactive, corrosive or foul-smelling. These items are excluded § 15 BOKraft Luggage and animals The passenger must stow them so they endanger nobody. Animals may not sit on the seats. § 15 BOKraft Passengers "},{"t":"Lesson 1.4: Starting a business: trade and commercial law","u":"lektion-1-4-unternehmen-gruenden.html","h":["Founding step by step","Am I a merchant (Kaufmann)?","Legal forms compared","Details that are often asked","Which legal form fits?","Typical mistakes","Summary"],"x":"Besides the licence under the PBefG, you must also register your business as a trade and choose a legal form. The legal form mainly decides whether you are liable with your private assets. Exam questions often ask about liability, minimum capital and the commercial register. Founding step by step A taxi or Mietwagen business is a trade ( Gewerbe ). You therefore need two things: the licence under the PBefG and the trade registration ( Gewerbeanzeige ) under the Trade Regulation Act. A typical order: Pass the exam and prove your equity ( lesson 1.2 ). Choose a legal form . For a GmbH or UG: articles of association at a notary, pay in capital, entry in the commercial register. Apply for the licence at the licensing authority. Register the trade at the municipal trade office ( Gewerbeamt ) § 14 GewO . The trade office informs the tax office, the IHK and the Berufsgenossenschaft, among others. Tax office: submit the tax registration questionnaire, receive a tax number. Berufsgenossenschaft: register the business with BG Verkehr within 1 week of starting § 192 SGB VII . With employees: apply for an employer number ( Betriebsnummer ) at the Federal Employment Agency and register staff with the health insurer ( lesson 1.8 ). Vehicles: register, insure, have the taximeter or odometer calibrated (module 3). With the trade registration you automatically become a member of the IHK . Prohibition of trade If a trader is unreliable, the authority can prohibit the trade fully or partly § 35 GewO . Unreliability is therefore doubly dangerous: for the licence and for the trade. Am I a merchant (Kaufmann)? The Commercial Code ( Handelsgesetzbuch, HGB ) applies to merchants . A merchant is anyone who runs a commercial business ( Handelsgewerbe ). Every trade is a commercial business unless , by its type or size, it does not need a business organised in a commercial way § 1 HGB . Small trade Merchant (Istkaufmann) Example One Mietwagen, the operator drives himself Business with 15 taxis, dispatch, employees, bookkeeping Commercial register not required, voluntary § 2 HGB mandatory § 29 HGB Name first and last name of the owner company name with suffix, e.g. “e. K.” (registered merchant) §§ 17, 19 HGB Bookkeeping usually cash-basis accounting (EÜR) double-entry bookkeeping under the HGB ( lesson 2.4 ) Whether a commercial organisation is needed depends, for example, on turnover, number of employees and vehicles and the number of transactions. The HGB has no fixed threshold. A GmbH or UG is always a merchant, however small. It is called a merchant by legal form ( Formkaufmann ). Legal forms compared Legal form Founders Minimum capital Liability Register Sole proprietorship Einzelunternehmen 1 person none unlimited , including private assets only as a merchant (e. K.) GbR (civil-law partnership) at least 2 none all partners unlimited and personally voluntary (company register, then “eGbR”) OHG (general partnership) at least 2 none all partners unlimited commercial register mandatory KG (limited partnership) at least 1 general partner + 1 limited partner none general partner ( Komplementär ) unlimited , limited partner ( Kommanditist ) only up to his contribution commercial register mandatory GmbH (limited company) at least 1 25.000 € , at least 12.500 € paid in at registration only the company's assets commercial register mandatory, notary UG (haftungsbeschränkt) at least 1 from 1 €, paid in in full in cash only the company's assets commercial register mandatory, notary § 705 BGB; §§ 105, 161 HGB; §§ 5, 5a, 7 GmbHG Details that are often asked GmbH: share capital ( Stammkapital ) at least 25.000 €. Before registration at least a quarter of each share must be paid in, in total at least half of the minimum capital, i.e. 12.500 € § 7 (2) GmbHG . The GmbH is run by the managing director ( Geschäftsführer ). UG (haftungsbeschränkt): a “mini GmbH” with less than 25.000 € capital. The capital must be paid in fully in cash ; contributions in kind (e.g. a car) are forbidden. Every year a quarter of the annual surplus must go into a reserve until 25.000 € are reached § 5a GmbHG . GmbH & Co. KG: a KG whose general partner is a GmbH. In the end no natural person is liable without limit. And the licence? With a GmbH or UG, the company is the operator and receives the licence. Professional competence must be held by a person who manages the business, for example the managing director § 13 (1) No. 3 PBefG . A company only receives the licence document once it is entered in the register § 15 (2) PBefG . Which legal form fits? One vehicle, you drive yourself Usually a sole proprietorship . Simple and cheap. But you are liable with everything you own. Two partners, small business GbR or, if the business is commercial, OHG . Both are fully liable. Several vehicles, employees, limit the risk GmbH or, with little starting capital, UG . But: notary, commercial register, balance sheet. That costs time and money. Limited liability has limits Even with a GmbH you are personally liable if, for example, you sign a personal guarantee ( Bürgschaft ) for a loan. Banks often ask for this from small companies. Typical mistakes ! “With the PBefG licence I don't need a trade registration.” You need both: the PBefG licence and the trade registration under § 14 GewO. ! “For a GmbH I must pay in 25.000 € immediately.” At least 12.500 € at registration (a quarter of each share, half in total). ! “For a UG I can contribute my car as capital.” Contributions in kind are forbidden for a UG. The capital must be fully paid in cash. ! “In a KG everyone is liable only up to their contribution.” Only the limited partner. The general partner is liable without limit. ! “In a GbR each partner is liable for half.” Each partner is liable without limit and personally for all debts. Summary Register trade office (§ 14 GewO), tax office, BG Verkehr within 1 week (§ 192 SGB VII), IHK membership automatic Merchant runs a commercial business (§ 1 HGB); small traders "},{"t":"Lesson 1.5: Drivers and road traffic law","u":"lektion-1-5-fahrpersonal-strassenverkehrsrecht.html","h":["The passenger transport driving permit","Requirements","Validity and renewal","Knowledge test instead of local knowledge test","Your duties as an operator","Conduct while on duty","Carrying children safely","The special rule for taxis","Seat belts and bus lanes","Seat belts also for taxi drivers","Bus lanes","Typical mistakes","Summary"],"x":"As an operator you are responsible for making sure only drivers with the right permit drive. On top of that come special traffic rules for taxis: child seats, seat belts, bus lanes, alcohol and smoking. Many exam questions are about the “P-Schein” and the child seat rule in taxis. The passenger transport driving permit Anyone who carries passengers in a taxi, Mietwagen or other vehicle requiring a PBefG licence needs a passenger transport driving permit ( Fahrerlaubnis zur Fahrgastbeförderung ) in addition to the normal driving licence. Colloquially it is called the “ P-Schein ” § 48 (1) FeV . This also applies to you as an operator if you drive yourself. Holders of a bus licence class D or D1 do not need a P-Schein § 48 (2) No. 4 FeV . Requirements Requirement Taxi / Mietwagen Driving licence for the vehicle usually class B Minimum age 21 years Driving experience class B for at least 2 years Reliability Certificate of good conduct ( Führungszeugnis ) and extract from the driver fitness register ( Fahreignungsregister , “Flensburg”) Health medical certificate of physical and mental fitness Annex 5 FeV Eyesight eye doctor or optician according to Annex 6 FeV Knowledge proof of professional knowledge, see below § 48 (4) FeV Validity and renewal The P-Schein is valid for at most 5 years . On application it is extended by up to 5 years each time if you again prove health, eyesight and reliability. From the age of 60 you also need proof of your performance (e.g. resilience, reaction) for a renewal. § 48 (5) and (6) FeV Knowledge test instead of local knowledge test Taxi drivers used to have to pass a local knowledge test ( Ortskundeprüfung ). It was abolished with the PBefG reform in August 2021. Instead § 48 FeV provides for proof of professional knowledge for drivers of taxis, Mietwagen and pooled on-demand transport, often called the “small knowledge test” ( kleine Fachkunde ). How and when it is required is decided by the federal states. Permits already issued remain valid. Ask your driving licence authority for the current situation. Don't confuse them The operator's exam ( Fachkundeprüfung für Unternehmer , this exam at the IHK) and the driver's knowledge test (for the P-Schein) are two different things. Your duties as an operator As the vehicle keeper ( Halter ) you may not order or allow passenger transport if the driver has no valid P-Schein § 48 (7) FeV . The driver must carry the P-Schein together with the driving licence and hand it over at checks § 48 (3) FeV . The authority revokes the P-Schein if a requirement is no longer met, for example after serious crimes § 48 (9) FeV . Practical tip Make a copy of every P-Schein and driving licence, note the expiry date and check the originals regularly. That way you notice in time when a permit expires. Conduct while on duty Drivers must behave considerately and calmly § 8 (1) BOKraft . For taxi and Mietwagen drivers also § 8 (3) and (5) BOKraft : No alcohol or other intoxicating substances while on duty or on standby. Anyone under their influence may not start a trip. This is stricter than the general 0.5 per mille limit. No watching TV while driving. Smoking is forbidden in taxis and Mietwagen. They count as public passenger transport §§ 1, 2 No. 2 BNichtrSchG . The driver may not hold the phone while driving. Using it in a holder or hands-free is allowed if only a short glance is needed § 23 (1a) StVO . Carrying children safely Children under 12 years who are smaller than 150 cm may only travel in a suitable, approved child restraint (child seat or booster seat) § 21 (1a) StVO . The special rule for taxis A taxi does not know in advance how many children will get in. So taxis (and other cars with an obligation to carry) have an easier rule: Taxi rule On the rear seats only 2 children weighing at least 9 kg must be able to be secured. Of these, at least 1 child weighing 9 to 18 kg must be able to be secured. This relief does not apply if children are carried regularly, for example on school runs. Then the full duty applies. In practice: a taxi should always carry at least one child seat for 9 to 18 kg and a second restraint (for example a booster seat). Another exception: children from 3 years may be secured with the normal belt on the rear seat if there is no space for another child seat because of other child seats § 21 (1a) StVO . Who is responsible? The driver is responsible for securing children correctly. Adult passengers must fasten their own seat belt. Seat belts and bus lanes Seat belts also for taxi drivers Taxi drivers used to be allowed to drive without a seat belt when carrying passengers. This exception no longer exists . Today taxi and Mietwagen drivers must also wear a seat belt while driving § 21a (1) StVO . Bus lanes A bus lane (sign 245, “BUS”) is for scheduled buses. Taxis may only use it if an additional sign allows it , for example “Taxi frei”. Without such a sign the bus lane is off-limits for taxis too. Typical mistakes ! “At 18 with a class B licence I can drive a taxi.” Minimum age 21 and class B for at least 2 years, plus the P-Schein. ! “The P-Schein is valid forever.” At most 5 years, then renewal with new proof. ! “Taxi drivers don't have to wear a seat belt.” This exception was abolished. Seat belts are mandatory. ! “I don't need a child seat in a taxi.” Restraints for at least 2 children from 9 kg, one of them 9 to 18 kg, must be possible. ! “0.3 per mille is fine on duty.” On duty: no alcohol (§ 8 BOKraft). Summary P-Schein needed for passenger transport under the PBefG (§ 48 (1) FeV); not with class D/D1 Requirements 21 years, class B for 2 years, certificate of good conduct + driver fitness register, health, eye test, knowledge Validity max. 5 years, renewal max. 5 years each; from 60 extra performance test Vehicle keeper must not allow any trip without a valid P-Schein Children in taxis rear seat: 2 children from 9 kg, one of them 9–18 kg securable; not for regular transport Seat belt mandatory for t"},{"t":"Lesson 1.6: Employment law: from contract to dismissal","u":"lektion-1-6-arbeitsrecht.html","h":["The employment contract","Written statement of the essential working conditions","Minimum wage, time recording, ID","Duties because of checks for undeclared work","Mini-job","Holidays and sick pay","Holidays","Sick pay","Fixed-term and part-time contracts","Dismissal","Notice periods","Summary dismissal","Protection Against Dismissal Act","Other protective laws","Typical mistakes","Summary"],"x":"As soon as you hire a driver, you are an employer. Then many laws apply: minimum wage, written proof of working conditions, holidays, sick pay and protection against dismissal. Customs checks the taxi trade particularly often because it counts as a sector with a risk of undeclared work. Mistakes quickly cost a lot of money here. The employment contract An employment contract ( Arbeitsvertrag ) is concluded when employer and employee agree. It can even be made orally. Still, you must record the most important conditions in writing (see below). Employee's duties Employer's duties Work , in person Pay wages , at least the minimum wage Follow instructions on content, place and time of work § 106 GewO Duty of care : protect health and safety Loyalty : keep business secrets, no competition Grant holidays, pay social insurance, issue a reference § 109 GewO Written statement of the essential working conditions The Act on Proof of Working Conditions ( Nachweisgesetz ) requires you to write down the main conditions, sign them and give them to the employee. There are three deadlines § 2 NachwG : At the latest … What? on the 1st working day names and addresses, pay (amount, supplements, due date, method of payment), working time, breaks, rest periods, and for shift work the shift system on the 7th calendar day start date, end date if fixed-term, place of work, job description, probation period, on-call work, overtime after 1 month holidays, training, company pension, dismissal procedure with notice periods and deadline for legal action, reference to collective agreements Special rule in the taxi trade In many sectors an email or PDF (text form) is now enough. This relief does not apply to the passenger transport trade . You must issue the statement on paper, sign it and hand it over § 2 (1) NachwG with § 2a SchwarzArbG . A written employment contract with all details replaces the statement. Minimum wage, time recording, ID From Minimum wage per hour (gross) 1 January 2026 13,90 € 1 January 2027 14,60 € § 1 MiLoG, Fifth Minimum Wage Adjustment Regulation (MiLoV5) The minimum wage ( Mindestlohn ) applies to every working hour . Waiting time at the taxi rank is also working time if the driver must be ready for customers there. Pure revenue-based pay (e.g. 40 % of revenue) is only allowed if at the end every hour is paid at least the minimum wage. Example (sample values) Driver works 160 hours in the month (incl. waiting time) Revenue share: 40 % of 5.200 € net revenue = 2.080,00 € Minimum wage: 160 h × 13,90 € = 2.224,00 € → You must pay at least 2.224,00 €, i.e. 144,00 € more. Duties because of checks for undeclared work The passenger transport trade is on the list of particularly checked sectors § 2a SchwarzArbG . The financial control unit of customs ( Finanzkontrolle Schwarzarbeit ) checks often. Therefore: ID: every driver must carry an ID card, passport or substitute document while working and show it to customs on request § 2a SchwarzArbG . Record working time: start, end and length of daily working time, at the latest by the 7th calendar day after the working day. Keep the records for at least 2 years § 17 (1) MiLoG . Immediate notification to social insurance at the latest when work starts ( lesson 1.8 ). Mini-job A mini-job ( geringfügige Beschäftigung ) exists if monthly earnings do not exceed the mini-job limit. The limit is linked to the minimum wage: in 2026 it is 603 € per month § 8 (1a) SGB IV . Minimum wage, holidays, sick pay and time recording also apply to mini-jobbers. Holidays and sick pay Holidays Statutory minimum holiday: 24 working days ( Werktage ) per year. Working days are Monday to Saturday. With a 5-day week that is 20 days , i.e. 4 weeks § 3 BUrlG . The full entitlement only arises after 6 months of employment (waiting period) § 4 BUrlG . Before that, 1/12 accrues for each full month. Holidays must be taken in the current year. They can only be carried over for urgent reasons, then until 31 March of the next year § 7 (3) BUrlG . Part-time holiday = 24 working days ÷ 6 × working days per week Example: a driver works 3 days a week. 24 ÷ 6 × 3 = 12 days of holiday . Sick pay If an employee is ill, the employer continues to pay wages for up to 6 weeks . After that the health insurer pays sickness benefit § 3 (1) EFZG . The entitlement only arises after 4 weeks of uninterrupted employment § 3 (3) EFZG . If the illness lasts longer than 3 calendar days , a doctor's certificate must be available, i.e. on day 4. The employer may ask for it earlier. Today you usually retrieve the sick note electronically from the health insurer (eAU) § 5 EFZG . Small businesses (usually up to 30 employees) take part in the U1 levy . The health insurer then refunds part of the sick pay ( lesson 1.8 ). Fixed-term and part-time contracts An employment contract can be fixed-term § 14 TzBfG : Without an objective reason: at most 2 years , with at most 3 extensions within that time. Not allowed if the employee has worked for you before. With an objective reason: for example covering for a sick driver. Then also longer. The fixed term must be agreed in writing and before work starts . Otherwise the contract is deemed open-ended §§ 14 (4), 16 TzBfG . Part-time employees may not be treated worse than full-time employees just because they work part-time § 4 TzBfG . Dismissal A notice of dismissal ( Kündigung ) must be in writing with a handwritten signature. Dismissal by email, text message or WhatsApp is invalid § 623 BGB . Notice periods Situation Notice period Probation period (max. 6 months) 2 weeks , to any day Basic period (for both sides) 4 weeks to the 15th or the end of a month Dismissal by the employer, employed for 2 years 1 month to the end of the month … for 5 / 8 / 10 years 2 / 3 / 4 months to the end of the month … for 12 / 15 / 20 years 5 / 6 / 7 months to the end of the month § 622 BGB The longer periods only apply if the employer gives notice. An employee can always give notice with the b"},{"t":"Lesson 1.7: Working time, breaks and rest periods","u":"lektion-1-7-arbeitszeit.html","h":["Who does the Working Hours Act apply to?","Maximum working time","Breaks","Rest period between two shifts","Shift checker","Sundays, public holidays and night work","Night work","Driving and rest times: do they apply to taxis?","Typical mistakes","Summary"],"x":"Driving a taxi often means long shifts, nights and weekends. For your employed drivers the Working Hours Act ( Arbeitszeitgesetz ) sets firm limits on working time, breaks and rest periods. Many confuse this with the EU driving time rules for lorries and buses. Those do not apply to taxis and Mietwagen. Who does the Working Hours Act apply to? The Working Hours Act (ArbZG) protects employees . It applies to your employed drivers and office staff, including mini-jobbers. It does not apply to you as a self-employed operator § 2 (2) ArbZG . Still: fatigue is one of the most common causes of accidents. Don't drive when you are tired. Working time is the time from the start to the end of work, without breaks § 2 (1) ArbZG . For taxis this includes waiting time at the rank, vehicle cleaning and cashing up. Maximum working time 8 hours per working day (Monday to Saturday) as a rule. up to 10 hours are allowed if an average of 8 hours per working day is not exceeded over 6 calendar months or 24 weeks . 48 hours per week on average (6 working days × 8 hours). § 3 ArbZG You must record working time over 8 hours per working day and keep the records for at least 2 years § 16 (2) ArbZG . In the taxi trade you must record all working time anyway because of § 17 MiLoG ( lesson 1.6 ). Breaks Working time per day Minimum break up to 6 hours not required more than 6 up to 9 hours 30 minutes more than 9 hours 45 minutes Breaks can be split into parts of at least 15 minutes . Nobody may work more than 6 hours in a row without a break. The break is fixed in advance. Waiting time at the taxi rank is not a break if the driver must be ready to leave at any time. § 4 ArbZG Rest period between two shifts After the end of work the employee needs an uninterrupted rest period of at least 11 hours § 5 (1) ArbZG . Exception for transport companies In transport companies ( Verkehrsbetriebe ), which includes taxi and Mietwagen businesses, the rest period may be shortened by up to 1 hour to 10 hours. Each shortening must be balanced within one calendar month or 4 weeks by extending another rest period to at least 12 hours § 5 (2) ArbZG . Shift checker Enter a shift. The checker calculates the working time and checks breaks and rest period under the ArbZG. Start of work End of work Ending after midnight is possible Total breaks min Start of the next shift Sundays, public holidays and night work In principle employees may not work on Sundays and public holidays § 9 ArbZG . Transport companies are an exception if the work cannot be done on working days § 10 (1) No. 10 ArbZG . So taxis may also run on Sundays. In return § 11 ArbZG : At least 15 Sundays per year must stay free. Anyone who works on a Sunday gets a substitute day off within 2 weeks . Anyone who works on a public holiday falling on a weekday gets a substitute day off within 8 weeks . Night work Night time is from 11 pm to 6 am . Night work is work that covers more than 2 hours of night time. For night workers §§ 2, 6 ArbZG : Working time in principle at most 8 hours; up to 10 hours only if balanced within 1 calendar month or 4 weeks. Right to an occupational health check (regularly every 3 years, from age 50 every year). Without a collective agreement: a reasonable number of paid days off or a reasonable pay supplement . Driving and rest times: do they apply to taxis? For lorries and buses there are EU rules on driving and rest times with a tachograph Reg. (EC) 561/2006 . In passenger transport they only apply to vehicles built for more than 9 persons including the driver . Remember for the exam Taxis and Mietwagen are cars with at most 9 seats including the driver. EU driving and rest times do not apply to them, and they need no tachograph . For employed drivers the Working Hours Act applies instead. If your business also runs minibuses with more than 9 seats (hired coaches), the EU rules, the Drivers Act (FPersG) and a separate licence apply to them. Typical mistakes ! “Taxi drivers must keep EU driving times and need a tachograph.” Only for vehicles for more than 9 people. For taxis the ArbZG applies. ! “With 8 hours of work, 15 minutes of break are enough.” More than 6 hours: 30 minutes; more than 9 hours: 45 minutes. ! “8 hours between two shifts are enough.” 11 hours; in transport companies 10 hours with compensation to 12 hours. ! “Working on Sunday gives no right to compensation.” Substitute day off within 2 weeks, 15 free Sundays per year. Summary Working time 8 h/working day, up to 10 h if averaged to 8 h over 6 months or 24 weeks (§ 3) Breaks over 6 h: 30 min; over 9 h: 45 min; parts of at least 15 min; max. 6 h in a row (§ 4) Rest period 11 h; transport company: 10 h with compensation to 12 h within 1 month/4 weeks (§ 5) Sunday allowed for transport companies (§ 10); 15 free Sundays; substitute day within 2 weeks (§ 11) Night 11 pm–6 am; night work = more than 2 h of it; supplement or days off (§ 6) EU driving times do not apply to taxi/Mietwagen (≤ 9 persons incl. driver); no tachograph"},{"t":"Lesson 1.8: Social insurance","u":"lektion-1-8-sozialversicherung.html","h":["The five branches","Notifications: immediate notification and registration","The immediate notification","The normal registration","Paying contributions","Mini-job and transition zone","And you yourself as the operator?","Bogus self-employment","Typical mistakes","Summary"],"x":"As an employer you register every driver for social insurance and pay contributions. The taxi trade has a particularly strict rule: the immediate notification ( Sofortmeldung ) before the first working day. And you yourself as an operator also have duties, for example towards the Berufsgenossenschaft. The five branches Branch Provider Rate 2026 Who pays? Health insurance Krankenversicherung health insurer (Krankenkasse) 14,6 % + additional rate (avg. 2,9 %) half each Long-term care Pflegeversicherung care insurer (at the Krankenkasse) 3,6 % half each; childless people from 23 pay a 0,6 % surcharge alone Pension Rentenversicherung Deutsche Rentenversicherung 18,6 % half each Unemployment Arbeitslosenversicherung Federal Employment Agency 2,6 % half each Accident Unfallversicherung Berufsgenossenschaft (for taxis: BG Verkehr) by risk class and payroll employer only Contributions are only calculated up to the contribution ceiling ( Beitragsbemessungsgrenze ). 2026: health and care 5.812,50 € per month, pension and unemployment 8.450 € per month. In addition there are levies paid only by the employer: U1 (refund for sickness, for businesses with usually up to 30 employees), U2 (refund for maternity, all businesses) and the insolvency levy . Remember Health, care, pension and unemployment insurance are paid jointly by employer and employee. Accident insurance is paid by the employer alone . Notifications: immediate notification and registration The employer registers employees with the health insurer . It is the collecting agency ( Einzugsstelle ) for all contributions except accident insurance. For mini-jobs the Minijob-Zentrale is responsible. First you need an employer number ( Betriebsnummer ) from the Federal Employment Agency. The immediate notification In the passenger transport trade you must report every new employee directly to the pension insurance data office at the latest when employment starts . This is the immediate notification ( Sofortmeldung ) § 28a (4) SGB IV . It applies to the same sectors as the ID duty under § 2a SchwarzArbG. Why so strict? At a customs check nobody should be able to say: “He's only starting today, the registration is coming.” Missing the immediate notification can lead to a fine. The normal registration In addition you register the employee with the health insurer with the first payroll , at the latest within 6 weeks of the start § 6 DEÜV . Further notifications follow when employment ends (deregistration) and once a year (annual notification). Paying contributions The employer deducts the employee's share from the wage and pays the total social insurance contribution to the health insurer. It is due on the third-to-last bank working day of the month in which the work was done § 23 (1) SGB IV . You send the contribution statement electronically beforehand. You pay accident insurance once a year to the Berufsgenossenschaft based on your payroll report. Mini-job and transition zone Mini-job Transition zone (“Midijob”) Earnings 2026 up to 603 € per month 603,01 € to 2.000 € per month Responsible Minijob-Zentrale health insurer Employer pays flat-rate contributions (health and pension, possibly flat-rate tax), levies, accident insurance normal contributions Employee pays small share of pension insurance (exemption on request possible) reduced contributions that rise gradually Immediate notification, minimum wage and time recording also apply to mini-jobbers. And you yourself as the operator? Accident insurance: mandatory Taxi and Mietwagen operators are insured themselves with BG Verkehr under its statutes, even without employees. The Federal Constitutional Court has confirmed this duty. Register the business with the BG within 1 week § 192 SGB VII . Health insurance: mandatory Everyone must have health insurance, statutory (voluntary) or private. Long-term care insurance comes with it. Pension insurance: usually voluntary But mandatory if you have no employees subject to insurance and work essentially for only one client (employee-like self-employed person) § 2 sentence 1 No. 9 SGB VI . Unemployment insurance: voluntary Possible on application if you were previously employed with insurance. Apply within 3 months of becoming self-employed § 28a SGB III . Careful with app trips If you drive alone with one Mietwagen and almost only take bookings from one single platform, have it checked whether you are subject to pension insurance as an employee-like self-employed person. Bogus self-employment Some operators use drivers as so-called “subcontractors” or “freelancers” to save on social contributions. If the driver actually works like an employee, he is bogus self-employed ( scheinselbständig ). Typical signs: He drives your vehicle, under your licence. You decide shifts and working hours. He has no business risk of his own and no customers of his own. Then he is an employee. You must pay the contributions retroactively, up to 4 years back, with intent up to 30 years , including the employee's share § 25 SGB IV . On top come late-payment surcharges and possibly criminal proceedings for withholding wages § 266a StGB . If in doubt, use the status determination procedure ( Statusfeststellungsverfahren ) at Deutsche Rentenversicherung § 7a SGB IV . Typical mistakes ! “I'll register the driver after the first month.” In the taxi trade: immediate notification at the latest when work starts. ! “Accident insurance is paid half each.” Only by the employer, to the Berufsgenossenschaft. ! “As a sole operator I don't need the BG.” Taxi and Mietwagen operators are insured with BG Verkehr under its statutes. ! “My drivers are subcontractors, so I pay no contributions.” Anyone who works like an employee is an employee. Back payment up to 4 years, with intent up to 30 years. Summary Five branches health, care, pension, unemployment (½ each), accident (employer only, BG) Rates 2026 health 14,6 % + avg. 2,9 %, care 3,6 %, pension 18,6 %, unemployment 2,6 % Collecting agency heal"},{"t":"Lesson 1.9: Contracts, the contract of carriage and liability","u":"lektion-1-9-vertraege-haftung.html","h":["How is a contract formed?","Legal capacity","Limitation period","The most important types of contract","Duties under the contract of carriage","Who is liable in an accident?","Maximum amounts of keeper's liability","Passengers are specially protected","Lost property in the taxi","Typical mistakes","Summary"],"x":"Every taxi trip is a contract. On top of that come contracts around the business: buying vehicles, leasing, renting, loans. In this lesson you learn the main types of contract, how a contract of carriage is formed and who is liable when something happens: accidents, damaged luggage and forgotten items. How is a contract formed? A contract is formed by two matching declarations: an offer and an acceptance §§ 145 ff. BGB . It does not have to be in writing. Clear conduct is also enough ( schlüssiges Verhalten ). Taxi example A passenger gets in at the taxi rank and says: “To the main station, please.” The driver starts the meter and drives off. A contract of carriage ( Beförderungsvertrag ) has been formed, without any signature. Legal capacity Children under 7 have no legal capacity . They cannot make contracts § 104 BGB . Minors aged 7 to 17 have limited capacity . Their contract is valid if the parents agree or if they pay immediately with their own pocket money §§ 106 ff., 110 BGB . Limitation period Claims usually become time-barred after 3 years . The period starts at the end of the year in which the claim arose §§ 195, 199 BGB . An unpaid invoice for a trip in May 2026 therefore becomes time-barred at the end of 31.12.2029. The most important types of contract Contract What happens? Example in the business Purchase Kaufvertrag § 433 BGB The seller hands over an item and transfers ownership, the buyer pays the price. Buying a taxi Rental Mietvertrag § 535 BGB Use of an item for a time against rent. Garage, office, renting a taximeter Lease Pachtvertrag § 581 BGB Use and the proceeds (“fruits”) against a lease payment. Leasing a whole business with its customers Leasing Leasingvertrag Not specifically regulated in the BGB, similar to rental. At the end the vehicle is returned or bought. Leasing a taxi Loan Darlehensvertrag § 488 BGB The bank provides money, the borrower pays interest and repays. Loan for a vehicle Contract for work Werkvertrag § 631 BGB A specific result is owed. Contract of carriage , repair at the garage Service / employment contract Dienst-/Arbeitsvertrag §§ 611, 611a BGB An activity is owed, not a result. Hiring a driver, tax adviser Remember The contract of carriage is a contract for work ( Werkvertrag ): the result is owed, i.e. safe arrival at the destination. The employment contract with the driver, by contrast, is a service contract. Duties under the contract of carriage The operator owes The passenger owes safe carriage to the agreed destination, by the shortest route § 38 BOKraft the fare according to the tariff or agreement a roadworthy, clean vehicle behaviour according to the conditions of carriage § 14 BOKraft carrying luggage within the usual limits compensation for damage he causes culpably (e.g. soiling) If a passenger refuses to pay, the driver can have his personal details taken and call the police . Anyone who takes a trip without intending to pay commits a criminal offence (fraud). Some tariffs allow an advance payment § 51 (1) sentence 2 No. 3 PBefG . Who is liable in an accident? In road traffic there are two kinds of liability: Keeper's liability (strict liability) Halterhaftung, Gefährdungshaftung The vehicle keeper is liable for damage caused by operating his vehicle, even without fault . Reason: a car is dangerous in itself. Excluded only in case of force majeure ( höhere Gewalt ). § 7 StVG Driver's liability The driver is liable if he caused the accident by fault. His fault is presumed. He must prove that he is not to blame. § 18 StVG Tort liability deliktische Haftung Anyone who intentionally or negligently injures another person's life, body, health or property is liable for damages without limit. § 823 BGB Maximum amounts of keeper's liability Personal injury per accident 5.000.000 € Property damage per accident 1.000.000 € for paid passenger transport, if more than 8 passengers are injured: for each further passenger additionally 600.000 € § 12 StVG With fault under § 823 BGB there is no upper limit. For all this your vehicle needs motor liability insurance ( lesson 2.5 ). Passengers are specially protected Personal injury: in paid, commercial passenger transport, keeper's liability towards passengers for death and injury may neither be excluded nor limited § 8a StVG . A sign saying “ride at your own risk” has no effect in a taxi. Property damage (e.g. luggage, laptop): the operator can only exclude his liability towards each passenger for the part above 1.000 € . Not even that is possible in case of intent or gross negligence § 23 PBefG . Example Through a minor driving error, a passenger's suitcase is destroyed. Damage: 1.600 €. The operator has excluded liability above 1.000 € in his conditions of carriage. He must pay 1.000 € . If the driver had been grossly negligent (e.g. ran a red light), he would have to pay the full 1.600 €. Lost property in the taxi After every trip the driver must check whether passengers have left anything behind § 11 BOKraft . If he cannot return it immediately, he hands it in without delay to the operator or the place named by the authority, for example the lost property office. If a passenger finds something in the taxi, he must hand it to the driver or operator § 978 BGB . Finder's reward: a passenger who finds something can receive a halved finder's reward from a value of 50 € . The driver as an employee of the company gets no finder's reward § 978 (2) BGB . Typical mistakes ! “Without a written contract there is no contract of carriage.” Getting in, naming the destination and driving off is enough (conduct). ! “The contract of carriage is a service contract.” It is a contract for work: the result is owed. ! “With a sticker ‘no liability for personal injury’ I'm protected.” Towards passengers in paid transport this has no effect (§ 8a StVG). ! “The driver gets a finder's reward for the phone he found.” Employees have no claim; passengers get a halved reward from 50 €. Summary Contract of carriage contr"},{"t":"Lesson 1.10: Taxes: VAT, receipts, vehicle, income and trade tax","u":"lektion-1-10-steuern.html","h":["Which taxes does a taxi business pay?","VAT: 7 % or 19 %?","Calculating VAT","Input VAT and VAT payable","VAT returns and small businesses","Advance VAT return","Small business rule","Receipt, invoice, till","The taxi receipt (up to 250 €)","Invoices over 250 €","E-invoice","The taximeter as a till: TSE and receipt duty","Income tax","Trade tax","Vehicle tax and wage tax","Vehicle tax","Wage tax","Typical mistakes","Summary"],"x":"Taxes are a big part of the exam. The most important is VAT ( Umsatzsteuer ): when does 7 % apply, when 19 %? What must be on a receipt? Then there are income, trade, vehicle and wage taxes. Many candidates calculate VAT wrongly in the exam. We practise that here. Which taxes does a taxi business pay? Tax On what? To whom? VAT Umsatzsteuer on every trip (ultimately paid by the passenger) tax office Income tax Einkommensteuer on profit (sole proprietorship, partnership) tax office Corporation tax Körperschaftsteuer on the profit of a GmbH or UG tax office Trade tax Gewerbesteuer on trade income municipality Vehicle tax Kfz-Steuer for every registered vehicle main customs office Wage tax Lohnsteuer on employees' wages (withheld by the employer) tax office VAT: 7 % or 19 %? The standard rate of 19 % applies to transport in principle § 12 (1) UStG . For taxis there is a reduced rate of 7 % if the trip § 12 (2) No. 10 UStG : stays within one municipality , or the distance is not more than 50 km . Mietwagen are not named in this provision. So the standard rate of 19 % applies to them, even for short trips. An exception exists for patient transport carried out by a Mietwagen on the same terms as a taxi, for example under an agreement with a health insurer that also applies to taxis. Trip VAT rate Taxi within Aachen, 12 km 7 % Taxi from Aachen to Düren, 34 km (different municipality, but ≤ 50 km) 7 % Taxi from Aachen to Cologne, 72 km 19 % Mietwagen within Aachen, 8 km 19 % Mietwagen to Düsseldorf airport, 85 km 19 % Remember 7 % only for taxis , and only within the municipality or up to 50 km. Mietwagen: 19 %. Calculating VAT The taxi tariff is a gross price . The VAT is already included. You must extract it: VAT = gross amount × rate ÷ (100 + rate) Net = gross amount ÷ (1 + rate) Example Taxi trip in town, passenger pays 21,40 € VAT = 21,40 € × 7 ÷ 107 = 1,40 € Net = 21,40 € ÷ 1,07 = 20,00 € Mietwagen trip, customer pays 59,50 € VAT = 59,50 € × 19 ÷ 119 = 9,50 € Net = 59,50 € ÷ 1,19 = 50,00 € Most common mistake 21,40 € × 7 % = 1,50 € is wrong . The 7 % refers to the net amount, not the gross amount. That is why you calculate × 7 ÷ 107. Input VAT and VAT payable When you buy things yourself (fuel, repairs, a new car), you also pay VAT. You may deduct this input VAT ( Vorsteuer ) from your own VAT if you have a proper invoice § 15 UStG . You only pay the difference to the tax office, the VAT payable ( Zahllast ). VAT payable = VAT collected − input VAT paid Example (sample values, one quarter) VAT from taxi trips (7 %) 2.450,00 € − input VAT on fuel (19 %) − 760,00 € − input VAT on garage work (19 %) − 190,00 € = VAT payable to the tax office 1.500,00 € Because taxis often collect only 7 % but pay 19 % input VAT on purchases, months with big purchases can even lead to a refund. VAT returns and small businesses Advance VAT return You report VAT electronically (ELSTER) and pay it by the 10th day after the end of the period § 18 (1) UStG . The period is normally the calendar quarter . If the tax in the previous year was over 9.000 € , you file monthly . If it was at most 2.000 € , the tax office can release you from advance returns § 18 (2) UStG . On application there is a permanent extension ( Dauerfristverlängerung ) by one month. After the year ends you file an annual VAT return . Small business rule With little turnover you can be a small business ( Kleinunternehmer ). Since 2025: turnover in the previous year at most 25.000 € and in the current year at most 100.000 € § 19 UStG . A small business charges no VAT and pays none. But it may also not deduct input VAT . It can waive the rule. Then it is bound for 5 years . For a taxi this means: the tariff is a final price. A small business keeps the full fare. For a full-time business, however, the limit is usually exceeded quickly. Receipt, invoice, till The taxi receipt (up to 250 €) For tax purposes a taxi receipt is a small-amount invoice ( Kleinbetragsrechnung ) if the amount is at most 250 € gross . It must contain § 33 UStDV : full name and address of the operator date of issue type and scope of the service (e.g. “taxi trip from … to …”) fare and tax in one sum (i.e. the gross amount) tax rate (7 % or 19 %) A taxi receipt is not a ticket ( Fahrausweis ) under § 34 UStDV. Tickets are, for example, train or bus tickets. Invoices for taxi or Mietwagen trips are not included. Invoices over 250 € From 250,01 € you need a full invoice with all details of § 14 (4) UStG, among others: name and address of you and the customer , your tax number or VAT ID , a sequential invoice number , date of service, net amount , tax rate and tax amount shown separately . E-invoice Between businesses (B2B) the electronic invoice is becoming mandatory step by step. Since 2025 all businesses must be able to receive e-invoices. Small-amount invoices up to 250 € are exempt. So a normal taxi receipt is still possible. The taximeter as a till: TSE and receipt duty For tax purposes taximeters and odometers count as electronic recording systems . Since 1 January 2026 every device must have a certified technical security device (TSE) § 146a AO, KassenSichV . Duty to issue receipts: for every trip a receipt must be created and offered to the passenger, on paper or electronically § 146a (2) AO . You must report the systems to the tax office via ELSTER. A breach is a tax offence. Income tax As a sole proprietor you pay income tax on your profit , not on turnover. You usually determine the profit with cash-basis accounting ( lesson 2.4 ). Basic tax-free allowance 2026: 12.348 €. Up to this amount you pay no income tax § 32a EStG . The rate rises with income, from 14 % to 42 % (45 % for very high incomes). The tax office sets advance payments . They are due on 10 March, 10 June, 10 September and 10 December § 37 EStG . A GmbH or UG pays corporation tax (15 % plus solidarity surcharge) and trade tax instead of income tax. Trade tax Trade tax goes to the municipality . It is calcul"},{"t":"Lesson 2.1: Payments, financing and key figures","u":"lektion-2-1-zahlungsverkehr-finanzierung.html","h":["How do passengers pay?","Transfers and direct debits","Reversing a direct debit","When a customer doesn't pay","Financing: where does the money come from?","The most important types of loan","Leasing","Collateral","Calculating interest and cash discounts","Is a cash discount worth it?","Is my business healthy? The key figures","Financial plan","Typical mistakes","Summary"],"x":"How do your passengers pay, and what does it cost you? How do you finance a new vehicle: loan or leasing? And how can you tell whether your business is healthy? This lesson covers payments, loans and collateral, and the most important key figures. The exam often has small calculations on interest, cash discounts (Skonto) and profitability. How do passengers pay? Payment method Advantage Disadvantage Cash available at once, no fees risk of robbery, change, counting, cash book Girocard / debit card safe, money in the account after 1–2 days card terminal, small fee per payment Credit card popular with tourists and business travellers higher fees (merchant discount) Mobile payment (phone, watch) fast, contactless fees like cards Payment via app no cash in the car, automatic billing platform commission, payout later Invoice (companies, health insurers) large customers, regular trips money comes later, risk of non-payment Whether a taxi must accept card payment can be set in the local tariff. The regulation may set the method of payment § 51 (1) sentence 2 No. 5 PBefG . In many cities card payment is now mandatory. Transfers and direct debits SEPA transfer Überweisung You instruct your bank to send money, using the payee's IBAN. Credited at the latest on the next bank working day. Instant transfer Echtzeitüberweisung Money reaches the payee within 10 seconds , around the clock. Since 9 October 2025 all banks in the euro area must offer it, and it may not cost more than a normal transfer. Reg. (EU) 2024/886 Verification of payee Since October 2025 the bank checks before every transfer whether name and IBAN match. If not, it warns you. SEPA direct debit Lastschrift The payee collects the money. For this he needs a written mandate from the payer. Good for regular payments, e.g. radio fee, insurance, vehicle tax. Reversing a direct debit SEPA core direct debit: the payer can reclaim an authorised debit within 8 weeks without giving reasons § 675x BGB . If there was no mandate at all (unauthorised), even up to 13 months § 676b BGB . SEPA business-to-business direct debit: only between businesses, no refund after debiting. When a customer doesn't pay A debtor is in default ( Verzug ) if he does not pay after a reminder, but at the latest 30 days after the due date and receipt of the invoice. For consumers this only applies if the invoice says so § 286 (3) BGB . You can then claim default interest : 5 percentage points above the base rate for consumers, 9 percentage points for businesses, plus a flat fee of 40 € for businesses § 288 BGB . If the customer still doesn't pay, the court dunning procedure ( Mahnbescheid ) helps. Financing: where does the money come from? Equity financing Debt financing Money from you, partners, retained profits bank, suppliers, leasing company Advantage no interest, independent large sums quickly, equity stays free Disadvantage limited interest, repayment, collateral, dependence The most important types of loan Type Explanation Typical use Overdraft Kontokorrentkredit credit line on the business account, flexible but expensive short-term gaps, e.g. until the health insurer pays Instalment loan Ratendarlehen equal repayment, interest falls over time buying a vehicle Annuity loan Annuitätendarlehen equal instalment (interest + repayment), repayment share rises buying a vehicle, business premises Bullet loan Fälligkeitsdarlehen only interest, repaid in one sum at the end rare in the taxi trade Supplier credit Lieferantenkredit payment term at the supplier (e.g. 30 days), often with a cash discount for quick payment garage, tyres, spare parts Guarantee credit Avalkredit the bank gives a guarantee for you rent deposit, security Documentary credit Akkreditiv the bank pays against certain documents foreign trade, hardly in the taxi trade Leasing With leasing you use a vehicle against monthly payments. The leasing company remains the owner. At the end you return it or buy it. Advantages: little own capital needed, payments are deductible business expenses, predictable costs, often with service. Disadvantages: usually more expensive overall than buying, mileage limit (important for taxis with high mileage!), extra payments for extra km or damage. Mileage leasing: fixed price for an agreed mileage. Residual value leasing: you bear the risk that the car is worth less at the end than planned. Collateral Collateral How it works Transfer of ownership as security Sicherungsübereignung The bank becomes owner of the vehicle, you may keep using it. The bank keeps the registration certificate part II (vehicle title). Retention of title Eigentumsvorbehalt The seller remains owner until everything is paid. Personal guarantee Bürgschaft A third person (e.g. spouse) is liable if you don't pay. Land charge / mortgage Grundschuld / Hypothek Security in a property, entered in the land register. Assignment Abtretung, Zession You assign receivables, e.g. against a health insurer. Pledge Verpfändung A pledge, e.g. a savings account. Calculating interest and cash discounts In commercial calculations the year has 360 days and each month 30 days. Interest = capital × rate × days ÷ (100 × 360) Example Overdraft 8.000 €, 11 % interest, 45 days Interest = 8.000 × 11 × 45 ÷ (100 × 360) = 110,00 € Is a cash discount worth it? An invoice says: “Payable within 30 days net, 2 % cash discount ( Skonto ) if paid within 10 days.” If you pay on day 10 you save 2 %, but give up 20 days of payment term. As an annual interest rate: Cash discount annual rate ≈ discount rate × 360 ÷ (payment term − discount period) 2 % × 360 ÷ (30 − 10) = 2 × 18 = 36 % per year Remember A cash discount is almost always a very good deal. It is even worth briefly using the overdraft (e.g. 11 %) for it. Is my business healthy? The key figures Key figure Formula What does it tell you? Equity ratio Eigenkapitalquote equity ÷ total capital × 100 How independent of banks am I? Return on equity Eigenkapitalrentabilität profit ÷ equity × 100 How"},{"t":"Lesson 2.2: Cost accounting","u":"lektion-2-2-kostenrechnung.html","h":["Why cost accounting?","Step 1: fixed or variable?","Step 2: depreciation and imputed interest","Depreciation","Imputed interest","Step 3: don't forget the owner's salary","Step 4: costs per km, per occupied km and per trip","Calculator with working","Contribution margin and break-even point","Mietwagen with an app: calculating commission correctly","Cost types, cost centres, cost units","Typical mistakes","Complete practice task"],"x":"Cost accounting ( Kostenrechnung ) is where most candidates fail. It is rarely the maths, but the structure: costs put in the wrong place, owner's salary forgotten, commission calculated wrongly. In this lesson you learn a fixed scheme that you can always apply the same way in the exam. Why cost accounting? As an operator you must know what one kilometre costs you. Only then do you know whether a trip pays off, whether the taxi tariff covers your costs and what price to charge for a Mietwagen trip. The exam almost always has a larger calculation task. It usually follows the same order: Collect the fixed costs ( Fixkosten ) per year, including depreciation, interest and owner's salary. Calculate the variable costs ( variable Kosten ) per kilometre. Total costs per year = fixed costs + variable costs × kilometres. Costs per unit : per km, per occupied km, per trip. From this derive the price, revenue or break-even point . Important In cost accounting always calculate with net amounts , i.e. without VAT. For you VAT is a pass-through item. More in lesson 1.10 . Step 1: fixed or variable? Fixed costs arise even if the car stays in the garage. Variable costs only arise with every kilometre driven or every euro of revenue. Fixed costs (per year) Variable costs (per km or % of revenue) Time-based depreciation Fuel or electricity Imputed interest Tyres Motor liability and comprehensive insurance Maintenance and repair by km Vehicle tax Oil, lubricants Radio or dispatch fee (flat rate) Usage-based depreciation (km) Driver wages with a fixed monthly salary Platform commission (% of revenue) Imputed owner's salary Revenue-based driver wages Administration, phone, garage, taximeter rent Rule of thumb Ask yourself: “Do I pay this even if I don't drive at all for a month?” Yes = fixed. No = variable. Step 2: depreciation and imputed interest Depreciation Your car loses value every year. You spread this loss of value as depreciation ( Abschreibung ) over the years of its useful life . In cost accounting you first deduct the residual value , i.e. the price you still get when selling. Depreciation per year = (purchase price − residual value) ÷ useful life Example (sample values) Purchase price 42.000 €, residual value 12.000 €, useful life 4 years. (42.000 − 12.000) ÷ 4 = 7.500 € per year There are two kinds: Time-based depreciation (per year): part of the fixed costs . This is the normal case in exam tasks. Usage-based depreciation (per km): part of the variable costs . Formula: (purchase price − residual value) ÷ total mileage in km. Read the task carefully. If it gives a mileage (“the vehicle should run 300.000 km”), usage-based depreciation is often meant. Cost accounting is not tax For tax purposes you depreciate a car according to the official depreciation table, without residual value. In cost accounting you use the real useful life and the residual value. The task tells you which is meant. Imputed interest You could also put the money tied up in the car in the bank. You include this lost interest as imputed interest ( kalkulatorische Zinsen ), even if you have no loan. Because the car is worth less each year, you use the average capital tied up : Imputed interest = (purchase price + residual value) ÷ 2 × interest rate Example (sample values) (42.000 + 12.000) ÷ 2 × 5 % = 27.000 × 0,05 = 1.350 € per year Even if you have a loan, you usually use imputed interest in cost accounting, not the actual loan interest. Otherwise you would count twice. Always follow the task. Step 3: don't forget the owner's salary If you drive yourself, the business pays you no wage. Still your work costs something. That is why you include an imputed owner's salary ( kalkulatorischer Unternehmerlohn ). It equals the salary you would get as an employee for the same work. Most common mistake Forgetting the owner's salary makes your costs too low. The price then covers the car, but not your work. In the exam this costs points. Step 4: costs per km, per occupied km and per trip Total costs = fixed costs + variable costs per km × km driven Cost per km = total costs ÷ km driven Cost per occupied km = total costs ÷ (km driven × occupied share) Cost per trip = total costs ÷ number of trips A taxi does not always have a passenger. The drive to the customer and the return to the rank or the office are empty kilometres . Only the occupied kilometres ( Besetztkilometer ) are paid. So the occupied kilometres must carry all costs. Remember The cost per occupied km is the figure that decides the price. It is always higher than the cost per km driven. Calculator with working Change the numbers. On the right you see the working as you should write it down in the exam. The error check shows typical problems. Taxi Mietwagen Sample values to try out, not real market prices. All amounts net. Vehicle Purchase price € Residual value at the end What you still get when selling € Useful life years Imputed interest rate % Fixed costs per year Insurance € Vehicle tax € Radio / dispatch (flat rate) € Other taximeter, administration, phone … € Driver wages incl. employer contributions 0 if you drive alone € Imputed owner's salary € Variable costs Consumption l/100 km Fuel price (net) €/l Set of tyres € Tyre mileage km Maintenance and repair €/km Platform commission share of revenue % Operation km driven per year km Occupied share share of km with a passenger % Trips per year trips Revenue per occupied km for contribution margin and break-even € 1 · Depreciation and interest 2 · Fixed costs per year 3 · Variable costs per km 4 · Result Error check Contribution margin and break-even point The contribution margin ( Deckungsbeitrag ) shows how much of every euro of revenue is left to pay the fixed costs. Contribution margin = revenue − variable costs As long as the contribution margin is positive, every trip helps to cover the fixed costs. Once all fixed costs are covered, profit begins. This point is called the break-even point ( Gewinnschwelle ). Break-e"},{"t":"Lesson 2.3: Calculating fares and quotes","u":"lektion-2-3-kalkulation.html","h":["The pricing scheme","What does each part of the taxi tariff cover?","Price floors","A quote for a Mietwagen trip","Reverse calculation: is the app price worth it?","Typical mistakes","Summary"],"x":"In lesson 2.2 you calculated your costs. Now you turn them into a price: for a quote to a company, an airport flat rate or a Mietwagen trip via an app. You learn the pricing scheme, the price floors and how to check whether a price set by an app covers your costs at all. The pricing scheme With forward calculation ( Vorwärtskalkulation ) you start from your costs and work out the price the customer pays: Cost price of the trip (from cost accounting) 100,00 € + profit mark-up (profit and risk), e.g. 12 % 12,00 € = net price 112,00 € + VAT (Mietwagen 19 %) 21,28 € = gross price (quote to private customers) 133,28 € To business customers you usually quote the net price plus VAT. Private customers must always be given the final price including VAT. Where does the cost price come from? From cost accounting ( lesson 2.2 ). Typically two rates: cost per km for the distance, including the empty return trip if there is one. cost per hour for waiting time, mainly driver's wage and owner's salary. What does each part of the taxi tariff cover? The taxi tariff is also a calculation. The authority sets it, but often on application by the trade with a cost calculation. Each component covers certain costs: Component mainly covers Base fare Grundpreis costs that arise for every trip regardless of length: driving to the customer, waiting at the rank, getting in and out, billing Price per km Kilometerpreis distance-related costs: fuel, tyres, maintenance, depreciation, and the empty kilometres that must be paid for too Time price / waiting Zeitpreis time-related costs: driver's wage and owner's salary when the taxi stands still or is in a traffic jam Surcharges Zuschläge special costs, e.g. a large vehicle, a lot of luggage If your costs rise, for example because of a higher minimum wage or more expensive fuel, the trade can apply to the authority for a tariff adjustment . Price floors Long-term price floor = cost price (all fixed and variable costs). Below it you make a loss in the long run. Short-term price floor = variable costs . Below it you lose additional money with every trip. Above it every trip contributes to the fixed costs (positive contribution margin ). Example (sample values) A hotel asks for a fixed price for trips to the station (6 km, with empty return 12 km). Your cost price: 0,95 € per km, of which 0,20 € per km variable. Cost price = 12 km × 0,95 € = 11,40 € → long-term floor (net) Variable costs = 12 km × 0,20 € = 2,40 € → short-term floor (net) The hotel offers 9,00 € net: → covers the variable costs (contribution margin 6,60 € per trip), but not the cost price. Only sensible to fill idle times. Careful with taxis Within the mandatory service area the tariff applies. You may only agree a fixed price with the hotel as a special agreement under § 51 (2) PBefG, or within a fixed price / price range for pre-booked trips ( lesson 1.3 ). A quote for a Mietwagen trip A company wants you to pick up a guest at Düsseldorf airport and bring him to Aachen. You calculate like this: Working (sample values) Empty drive Aachen → Düsseldorf 85 km Trip with guest Düsseldorf → Aachen 85 km Total km 170 km Cost per km (incl. driver) 1,10 € Waiting at the airport (delay) 1 h × 32,00 € Distance costs = 170 km × 1,10 € = 187,00 € Waiting time = 1 h × 32,00 € = 32,00 € Cost price = 219,00 € + profit mark-up 15 % = 32,85 € Net price = 251,85 € + 19 % VAT = 47,85 € Gross price = 299,70 € Common mistakes: forgetting the empty trip , calculating with 7 % instead of 19 % (Mietwagen!), or adding the mark-up to the gross price. Practise more quotes in the calculation trainer . Reverse calculation: is the app price worth it? For many app trips the platform sets the price. Then you calculate backwards ( Rückwärtskalkulation ): what is left for you after VAT and commission? Example (sample values) The passenger pays 23,80 € in the app for 9 km. Plus 3 km to drive to the customer. In this example the platform keeps 25 % of the net price. Your costs: 0,90 € per km driven. Gross price 23,80 € − 19 % VAT (23,80 × 19 ÷ 119) − 3,80 € = Net price 20,00 € − 25 % commission − 5,00 € = Revenue for you 15,00 € Costs: (9 km + 3 km approach) × 0,90 € = 10,80 € Result: 15,00 € − 10,80 € = + 4,20 € per trip ✓ Always count all km: the drive to the customer and, if no follow-up booking comes, the return to the office (duty to return!). Check your contract Platforms calculate the commission differently, sometimes on the net, sometimes on the gross price, sometimes with fixed fees. In the exam, the task tells you how to calculate. Typical mistakes ! Profit mark-up added to the gross price. Order: cost price → + profit → net → + VAT → gross. ! Empty drive to the pick-up forgotten. All km driven count, also without a passenger. ! Mietwagen priced with 7 % VAT. Mietwagen: 19 %. ! “I never drive below cost price.” Short term a trip above variable costs can make sense. Long term all costs must be covered. Summary Forward cost price + mark-up = net; + VAT = gross Reverse gross − VAT = net; − commission = revenue; compare with costs Tariff base fare: costs per trip; km price: distance incl. empty km; time price: wages when standing Price floors long-term: cost price; short-term: variable costs Taxi deviation from tariff only as a special agreement or fixed price/range per tariff"},{"t":"Lesson 2.4: Bookkeeping, cash book and profit calculation","u":"lektion-2-4-buchfuehrung.html","h":["Who must keep which books?","Retention periods","The cash book","The most important rules","Shift sheets","Cash-basis accounting (EÜR)","Purchases: depreciation instead of an immediate expense","Basics of double-entry bookkeeping","Typical mistakes","Summary"],"x":"The tax office looks particularly closely at the taxi trade because a lot is paid in cash. You must record your income completely, keep a cash book and calculate your profit correctly. In this lesson you learn who must keep which books, how long to keep what, how a cash book works and how cash-basis accounting ( Einnahmen-Überschuss-Rechnung ) is structured. Who must keep which books? Double-entry bookkeeping (balance sheet) Cash-basis accounting (EÜR) Who? merchants under the HGB (e.g. GmbH, UG, OHG, KG, e. K.) § 238 HGB, § 140 AO ; also traders above the limits of § 141 AO everyone else: small traders who are not merchants and stay below the limits § 4 (3) EStG Limits § 141 AO Bookkeeping duty if turnover over 800.000 € or profit over 80.000 € per year. The tax office notifies you of the duty. Sole merchants below these limits are also exempt under the HGB § 241a HGB . Profit comparison of assets: balance sheet and profit and loss account business income − business expenses Effort high, usually with a tax adviser manageable, can be done yourself For all operators: record turnover for VAT § 22 UStG , and record cash receipts individually § 146 (1) AO . The principles for proper digital bookkeeping are in the GoBD : complete, correct, timely, orderly and unalterable. Retention periods Documents Period books, records, inventories, annual accounts, opening balance sheet 10 years accounting vouchers : invoices, receipts, bank statements, taximeter printouts 8 years business letters received and sent, other tax-relevant documents 6 years § 147 (1) and (3) AO The period starts at the end of the calendar year in which the document was created § 147 (4) AO . A receipt from 3 February 2026 may therefore be destroyed on 1 January 2035 at the earliest (end of 2026 + 8 years). Digital data must remain readable and available for an audit during the whole period. Don't confuse Bookings in Mietwagen transport: 1 year (§ 49 (4) PBefG). Working time records: 2 years (§ 17 MiLoG). Tax vouchers: 8 years , books 10 years . The cash book Anyone who takes a lot of cash needs a cash book ( Kassenbuch ). It contains all cash receipts and payments with date and voucher, and after each day the cash balance. The most important rules Enter daily and promptly, in chronological order. Individual records : every trip separately. In a taxi the taximeter with TSE does this ( lesson 1.10 ). No negative cash balance . You cannot take more out of a till than is in it. A negative balance tells an auditor at once: income is missing here. Ready for a cash count ( kassensturzfähig ): the counted cash must match the book at any time. No later changes that can no longer be seen. No correction fluid, no spreadsheet that can be freely overwritten. Also enter private withdrawals and private deposits. Shift sheets For every shift the driver records: date, driver, vehicle, mileage at start and end, occupied km, number of trips and receipts (cash, card, invoice). These shift sheets ( Schichtzettel ) and the taximeter data are primary records and must be kept. Exercise: cash book (sample values) Date Text Receipt Payment Balance 01.03. Opening balance 150,00 01.03. Cash receipts shift (per taximeter) 312,40 462,40 01.03. Petrol station, voucher 17 78,50 383,90 01.03. Paid into bank 250,00 133,90 02.03. Cash receipts shift 268,10 ? Cash-basis accounting (EÜR) Profit = business income − business expenses Cash principle: income counts when the money arrives. Expenses count when they are paid § 11 EStG . An invoice to a health insurer from December that is paid in January is income in January. Exception: regularly recurring payments (e.g. VAT advance payments, rent) made up to 10 days before or after the turn of the year count for the year they belong to. The EÜR is submitted electronically to the tax office with the form Anlage EÜR . VAT: VAT collected is business income; input VAT paid and VAT paid to the tax office are business expenses. Purchases: depreciation instead of an immediate expense A new taxi is not a business expense in one year. The costs are spread over the useful life: depreciation (for tax: AfA ) § 7 EStG . Useful life according to the official depreciation table . For cars in taxi and Mietwagen transport the sector table for passenger transport applies: 5 years . For tax purposes there is no residual value. In the year of purchase only pro rata for the full months from the month of purchase. Low-value assets up to 800 € net (e.g. a card terminal) may be deducted in full immediately § 6 (2) EStG . EÜR example (sample values, one taxi, operator drives alone) Business income fares net 58.000 € VAT collected (7 %) 4.060 € total 62.060 € Business expenses fuel (net) 9.200 € input VAT (total) 2.550 € VAT paid to tax office 1.510 € insurance 4.800 € vehicle tax 180 € radio / dispatch 1.800 € garage, tyres 3.100 € depreciation taxi (45.000 € ÷ 5 years) 9.000 € other 1.400 € total 33.540 € Profit 28.520 € The imputed owner's salary from cost accounting does not appear in the EÜR. Your “wage” is the profit. Basics of double-entry bookkeeping Stocktaking Inventur Counting, measuring, weighing all assets and debts on a key date (usually 31.12.). Inventory Inventar The result of stocktaking: detailed list of assets and debts. Balance sheet Bilanz Short comparison: on the left assets (vehicles, cash, bank, receivables), on the right liabilities and equity (equity, debts). Both sides are equal. P&L GuV Profit and loss account: income minus expenses of the year. Journal Grundbuch All entries in chronological order. General ledger Hauptbuch The same entries sorted by account. Account Konto T-shaped record with debit ( Soll , left) and credit ( Haben , right). Booking entry Buchungssatz “Debit to credit” ( Soll an Haben ). Every entry touches at least two accounts. Examples of booking entries Fuel paid in cash, 50 € net + 9,50 € VAT: Fuel costs 50,00 / Input VAT 9,50 to Cash 59,50 Cash receipts of a shift 107 € gross (7 %): Cash 107,00 "},{"t":"Lesson 2.5: Insurance","u":"lektion-2-5-versicherungen.html","h":["Mandatory insurance","Minimum sums insured for motor liability","Sensible voluntary insurance","And for you privately as the owner","Your duties towards the insurer","How much can motor liability insurance reclaim?","Typical mistakes","Summary"],"x":"A single serious accident can ruin a small taxi business if insurance is missing or doesn't pay. Some insurance is mandatory, other insurance is very sensible. Just as important are your duties towards the insurer. If you break them, you may have to pay yourself. Mandatory insurance Motor liability insurance Kfz-Haftpflichtversicherung Mandatory for every vehicle. It pays for damage you cause to others with the vehicle, including your passengers. No registration without it. § 1 PflVG Statutory accident insurance Via the Berufsgenossenschaft (BG Verkehr). For employees and for you as a taxi or Mietwagen operator (statutes). Covers accidents at work and on the way to work. SGB VII Employees' social insurance Health, care, pension, unemployment insurance ( lesson 1.8 ). The operator's health insurance Everyone must have health insurance, statutory or private. Minimum sums insured for motor liability Type of damage (per accident) At least Personal injury 7.500.000 € Property damage 1.300.000 € Pure financial loss 50.000 € Annex to § 4 (2) PflVG For vehicles with more than 9 seats (excluding the driver's seat) the sums increase per additional seat. For normal taxis and Mietwagen the basic amounts apply. In practice most operators agree much higher sums, often a flat 100 million euros. Direct claim An injured party can turn directly to your motor liability insurer § 115 VVG . State the correct use The vehicle must be insured as a taxi or Mietwagen . The premiums are higher than for private cars. Anyone who insures a vehicle as a private car and carries passengers commercially breaks a contractual duty (use clause). The insurer still pays the other party, but can reclaim money from you. Sensible voluntary insurance Insurance What it pays For taxi/Mietwagen Partial cover Teilkasko own vehicle damage from theft, fire, glass breakage, storm, hail, collision with animals recommended Comprehensive Vollkasko also own-fault accidents and vandalism for new or financed vehicles often required by the bank or leasing company Business liability Betriebshaftpflicht damage not caused while driving, e.g. a passenger trips over luggage in the office recommended Passenger accident insurance fixed sums if occupants are injured, whoever is at fault possible, often replaced by driver protection Driver protection insurance Fahrerschutz personal injury of your own driver in an own-fault accident sensible; motor liability does not pay for this Legal expenses Rechtsschutz lawyer and court costs (traffic, company, employment law) recommended Business interruption loss of income if the taxi is off the road after damage sensible for small businesses Property insurance building, office equipment, electronics (taximeter, radio), inventory depending on the business And for you privately as the owner Health insurance with daily sickness benefit : as a self-employed person you get no sick pay. Occupational disability insurance : if you can no longer drive, your income is gone. Retirement provision : voluntary pension insurance or private provision. Long-term care insurance : mandatory, part of health insurance. Your duties towards the insurer Contractual duties ( Obliegenheiten ) are rules of conduct from the insurance contract. Breaking them risks the insurer paying nothing or only part, or reclaiming money (recourse). Before the damage (examples) After the damage (examples) Use the vehicle only for the agreed purpose (taxi/Mietwagen) Report the damage without delay Don't let anyone drive without a licence No hit-and-run, stay at the scene Don't let anyone drive under alcohol or drugs Give truthful and complete information Don't allow unauthorised use Keep the damage as small as possible §§ 5, 6 KfzPflVV How much can motor liability insurance reclaim? The liability insurer always pays the other party. But it can reclaim money from you. In motor liability this is limited: Breach of a duty before the damage (e.g. drunk driver, wrong use) up to 5.000 € per person Breach of a duty after the damage (intentional or grossly negligent) up to 2.500 € particularly serious intentional breach of the duty to clarify after the damage (e.g. hit-and-run) up to 5.000 € § 5 (3), § 6 KfzPflVV In comprehensive insurance these limits don't exist. There the insurer can reduce payment for gross negligence and refuse it entirely for intent. Typical mistakes ! “Motor liability also pays for damage to my own taxi.” No. Own vehicle damage: comprehensive/partial cover (Kasko). ! “I drive Mietwagen with my private car insurance.” The vehicle must be insured for commercial passenger transport. ! “Minimum cover for personal injury: 5 m €.” 5 m € is the liability limit under § 12 StVG. The minimum sum insured is 7,5 m €. ! “After a hit-and-run the liability insurer pays the victim nothing.” It pays the victim. It can reclaim up to 5.000 € from the insured. Summary Mandatory motor liability, statutory accident insurance (BG), employees' social insurance, own health insurance Minimum sums 7,5 m € personal, 1,3 m € property, 50.000 € financial loss Voluntary partial/comprehensive cover, driver protection, business liability, legal expenses, business interruption, daily sickness benefit, disability Duties before damage: use, licence, no alcohol; after damage: report, no hit-and-run, information Recourse (liability) max. 5.000 € (before), 2.500 € / 5.000 € (after)"},{"t":"Lesson 2.6: Documents to carry and retention periods","u":"lektion-2-6-dokumente.html","h":["The checklist for every trip","Marking on the vehicle","Digital instead of paper","Repetition: how long to keep?","Typical mistakes","Summary"],"x":"At a check by the police, customs or public order office, the driver must be able to show certain documents immediately. If something is missing, there are fines, for the driver and often for you as the operator. This lesson is short but popular in the exam: which document belongs to the driver, which to the vehicle, which to the company? The checklist for every trip Document Relates to Legal basis Driving licence ( Führerschein , class B) driver § 4 FeV Passenger transport driving permit (P-Schein), together with the licence, hand over on request driver § 48 (3) FeV ID card, passport or substitute document, show to customs on request driver § 2a SchwarzArbG Registration certificate part I ( Zulassungsbescheinigung Teil I ), on paper or digitally in the i-Kfz app vehicle FZV Licence document or shortened official copy , in writing or electronically company § 17 (4) PBefG Rules on fares and conditions of carriage , in a taxi the valid taxi tariff; passengers may look at it company § 10 BOKraft For Mietwagen: proof that the booking came via the registered office (e.g. in the app or on the booking note) company § 49 (4) PBefG Memory aid L icence, P -Schein, I D, R egistration, P ermit (Genehmigung), T ariff. In German: F-P-A-Z-G-T (Führerschein, P-Schein, Ausweis, Zulassung, Genehmigung, Tarif). Not documents, but also mandatory in the vehicle: first-aid kit, warning triangle, high-visibility vest and in taxis the child seats ( lesson 3.3 , lesson 1.5 ). Marking on the vehicle Taxi Mietwagen Licence number Ordnungsnummer sign with the licence number issued by the authority at the bottom right of the rear window , readable from inside and outside also at the bottom right of the rear window (own design) Operator's address sign with the name and registered office of the operator inside, clearly visible to the passenger not required § 27 BOKraft This way a passenger can identify the vehicle, for example if he has forgotten something or wants to complain. Digital instead of paper The licence or its shortened copy may also be carried electronically § 17 (4) PBefG . Since late 2025 the vehicle registration certificate is also available digitally in the government i-Kfz app . Showing it in the app fulfils the duty to carry it. Driving licence, P-Schein and ID must still be carried as originals . In practice Keep a folder for each vehicle with the licence copy, tariff and registration certificate. At every driver change, check that the driver has his licence, P-Schein and ID. Repetition: how long to keep? Booking records in Mietwagen transport 1 year Working time records (MiLoG, ArbZG) 2 years Business letters 6 years Accounting vouchers (receipts, invoices, taximeter data) 8 years Books, cash book, annual accounts 10 years Typical mistakes ! “The licence document is in the office.” In the vehicle: the document or a shortened official copy. ! “A photo of the P-Schein on my phone is enough.” The P-Schein must be carried and handed over as an original. ! “The driver doesn't need his ID, he has his driving licence.” In the passenger transport trade the ID duty under § 2a SchwarzArbG applies. Summary Driver driving licence, P-Schein, ID card or passport Vehicle registration certificate part I (paper or i-Kfz app); licence number on the rear window Company licence or shortened official copy; tariff and conditions of carriage; taxi: operator's address inside"},{"t":"Lesson 3.1: Vehicle registration and operation","u":"lektion-3-1-zulassung.html","h":["Operating permit and registration","Documents for registration","From new car to taxi: the steps","Changing vehicle, changing keeper, deregistration","Typical mistakes","Summary"],"x":"Before a new vehicle may drive as a taxi or Mietwagen, it needs more than number plates: an operating permit, registration, a special general inspection under BOKraft, an entry in your licence and a calibrated taximeter or distance meter. Here you learn the correct order. Operating permit and registration Operating permit / type approval Betriebserlaubnis / Typgenehmigung Confirms that the vehicle model meets the technical rules. New cars have an EU type approval; the manufacturer issues the certificate of conformity (COC) . Individually converted or imported vehicles need an individual inspection. § 19 StVZO Registration Zulassung The registration authority ( Straßenverkehrsamt of the district or city) allows this particular vehicle to drive on public roads. It gets a number plate and the registration certificates. FZV Documents for registration Insurance confirmation as an electronic number ( eVB ), for use as a taxi or Mietwagen Registration certificate part II (vehicle title) or COC for new cars ID card or passport; for companies an extract from the commercial register and the trade registration SEPA direct debit mandate for vehicle tax for used cars: proof of the last general inspection Many registrations can also be done online today (i-Kfz). Registration certificate part I Registration certificate part II formerly Fahrzeugschein formerly Fahrzeugbrief (vehicle title) belongs in the vehicle (or digitally in the app) belongs safely in the office , never in the car proof of registration at checks proof of the right to dispose of the vehicle; with financing the bank often keeps it From new car to taxi: the steps Choose the vehicle : at least two axles and four wheels § 17 BOKraft , at least two doors on the right-hand side § 25 BOKraft . The authority can also set requirements on emissions and accessibility § 13 (5b) PBefG . Equip it : taxi: paint or foil in RAL 1015, taxi sign, taximeter with TSE, navigation device, alarm system. Mietwagen: distance meter with TSE, alarm system, no taxi sign ( lesson 3.3 ). Insure it as a taxi or Mietwagen and get the eVB number. Register it with the registration authority. Calibration ( Eichung ) of the taximeter or distance meter in the vehicle by the calibration office. New devices first go through a conformity assessment. General inspection under BOKraft before the first use, at your own cost. The inspector also checks whether the vehicle complies with BOKraft. You give the report to the licensing authority without delay § 42 BOKraft . Licensing authority : the number plate is entered in the licence document; you receive the licence number ( Ordnungsnummer ) and a copy for the vehicle § 17 PBefG, § 27 BOKraft . Tax : report the taximeter/distance meter to the tax office as an electronic recording system ( lesson 1.10 ). Only then drive Anyone who carries passengers in a vehicle that is not in the licence drives without a licence. Fine up to 20.000 € ( lesson 1.2 ). Changing vehicle, changing keeper, deregistration Changing the vehicle : the new vehicle must be entered in the licence document. Present the document to the licensing authority for amendment § 17 (2) PBefG . A pure vehicle exchange does not need a new hearing § 14 (3) PBefG . Sale : the seller reports the new keeper to the registration authority. The new keeper must re-register the vehicle. Before that: remove the taxi sign, remove or unseal the taximeter, change the taxi paint if necessary. Deregistration ( Außerbetriebsetzung ): at the registration authority, the plates are de-stamped. Insurance and vehicle tax end. Tell the licensing authority that the vehicle is no longer used. Change of name or address : report it to the registration authority and inform the licensing authority § 54 (2) PBefG . Typical mistakes ! “The vehicle title is in the glove box.” Part II belongs in the office. Only part I goes in the car. ! “The new taxi is registered, so I'll start driving.” First: calibration, BOKraft inspection, entry in the licence. ! “Registration and operating permit are the same thing.” Operating permit: the vehicle type is technically fine. Registration: this vehicle may go on the road. Summary Registration eVB, part II/COC, ID, SEPA mandate for vehicle tax; authority: Straßenverkehrsamt Part I / part II part I in the vehicle, part II in the office Before first use equipment, calibration, inspection under § 42 BOKraft with report to the authority, entry in the licence, licence number Vehicle change present the licence document for amendment (§ 17 (2) PBefG)"},{"t":"Lesson 3.2: Maintenance, roadworthiness test and calibration","u":"lektion-3-2-wartung-hu.html","h":["General inspection","Calibration of taximeter and distance meter","When must it be recalibrated earlier?","A maintenance plan","Typical mistakes","Summary"],"x":"Taxis often drive 60.000 to 100.000 km a year. That wears out brakes, tyres and suspension quickly. This is why taxis and Mietwagen have shorter inspection intervals than private cars: a general inspection and taximeter calibration every year. As an operator you must also be able to draw up a maintenance plan. General inspection The general inspection is called Hauptuntersuchung (HU), often simply “TÜV”. Vehicle HU every private car 24 months (new: first after 36) Car for passenger transport under the PBefG (taxi, Mietwagen) 12 months § 29 StVZO, Annex VIII No. 2.1 The HU is carried out by inspection engineers from TÜV, DEKRA, GTÜ, KÜS and other inspection organisations. The emissions test is part of the HU. For taxis and Mietwagen the inspector also checks whether the vehicle complies with BOKraft: taxi sign, alarm system, doors, licence number, etc. § 41 BOKraft The licensing authority receives a copy of the report without delay § 41 BOKraft . Before the first use in the business an HU under BOKraft is required, even for a new car § 42 BOKraft . If you exceed the deadline by more than 2 months , you get a more expensive extended inspection. Driving with an expired HU also costs a fine. Calibration of taximeter and distance meter Device Calibration interval Taximeter ( Fahrpreisanzeiger ) 1 year Distance meter ( Wegstreckenzähler , Mietwagen) 2 years MessEV Annex 7 The responsible body is the state calibration office ( Eichamt ). Apply in good time; many offices want the application several weeks before the deadline. When must it be recalibrated earlier? after a repair or any intervention in the device, after reprogramming , for example after a tariff change, when the device is installed in a different vehicle , when the seal is damaged. Without valid calibration A taximeter without valid calibration must not be used. This breaks calibration law and BOKraft (§ 28 BOKraft: “the provisions of calibration law apply”). A maintenance plan The DIHK framework requires that you can draw up a maintenance plan for your vehicles. Plan maintenance by kilometres and by time , whichever comes first. When? What? Who? Before every shift Pre-departure check: lights, tyres, brakes, mirrors, windows, cleanliness, taximeter, child seats, warning triangle, first-aid kit ( lesson 4.1 ) driver weekly tyre pressure, oil level, coolant, washer fluid, interior cleaning driver / business according to the manufacturer (e.g. every 20.000–30.000 km) service: oil, filters, brakes, suspension garage seasonal summer/winter tyre change, air conditioning, battery in autumn garage yearly general inspection with emissions test, taximeter calibration inspection organisation, calibration office every 2 years distance meter calibration (Mietwagen) calibration office Keep a maintenance log or list for each vehicle: date, mileage, work done, next due date. This also helps when selling and in disputes with the insurer. Why is it worth it? A taxi that is off the road for a week because of a defect earns no money but still costs fixed costs. Preventive maintenance is usually cheaper than a breakdown. Typical mistakes ! “My taxi has an HU every 2 years like a private car.” Taxi and Mietwagen: every 12 months. ! “The distance meter is calibrated every year.” Taximeter: 1 year. Distance meter: 2 years. ! “After the tariff change I can reset the taximeter myself.” A specialist workshop reprograms it, then it must be calibrated. Summary HU taxi/Mietwagen every 12 months; also checks BOKraft; report to the licensing authority Before first use HU under § 42 BOKraft Calibration taximeter 1 year, distance meter 2 years; again after repair, reprogramming, installation in another vehicle Maintenance pre-departure check, service by km/time, maintenance log"},{"t":"Lesson 3.3: Vehicle equipment and design","u":"lektion-3-3-ausruestung.html","h":["Taxi and Mietwagen compared","Mandatory equipment for every car","Tyres and winter equipment","Typical mistakes","Summary"],"x":"A taxi is more than a car with a yellow sign. BOKraft states exactly how taxis and Mietwagen must be built and equipped: doors, alarm system, markings, measuring device, navigation and luggage space. On top of that comes the general mandatory equipment of every car. The exam often asks what a taxi has and a Mietwagen doesn't. Taxi and Mietwagen compared Equipment Taxi Mietwagen Rule at least 2 axles and 4 wheels ✓ ✓ § 17 BOKraft at least 2 doors on the right-hand side ✓ ✓ § 25 (1) Alarm system , triggered from the driver's seat: horn sounds at intervals, headlights and rear indicators flash ✓ ✓ § 25 (2) Partition between driver and passengers (then sufficiently bullet-resistant) allowed allowed § 25 (3) Colour light ivory RAL 1015 ✓ – § 26 (1) illuminated taxi sign ( Taxischild ) on the roof ✓ forbidden §§ 26, 31 Advertising only on the side doors ✓ ✓ § 26 (2) Licence number on the rear window ✓ ✓ § 27 Sign with name and registered office inside ✓ – § 27 calibrated, illuminated taximeter ✓ – § 28 Navigation device with real-time traffic data and traffic-jam avoidance ✓ – § 28a Space for at least 50 kg of luggage even when fully occupied ✓ – § 29 calibrated distance meter – ✓ § 30 Of the special rules, only §§ 25, 27 and 30 BOKraft apply to Mietwagen § 31 BOKraft . The federal states can allow exceptions for some points, for example the colour § 43 BOKraft . One vehicle for both? If a vehicle has a taxi licence and also a licence for another type of transport, the taxi rules §§ 25 to 30 apply. While it is driving as a Mietwagen, the taxi sign must not be used § 31 BOKraft . Mandatory equipment for every car First-aid kit according to DIN 13164, not expired § 35h StVZO Warning triangle type-approved § 53a (1) StVZO High-visibility vest at least one, within reach § 53a (2) StVZO For taxis the child seats are also required (at least for 2 children from 9 kg, one of them for 9 to 18 kg, see lesson 1.5 ). A fire extinguisher is mandatory for buses but not for cars; it is still recommended. Tyres and winter equipment Minimum tread depth : 1,6 mm § 36 (2) StVZO . Recommended: 3 mm in summer, 4 mm in winter, because the braking distance gets much longer with little tread. Situational winter tyre duty : in black ice, snow, slush, ice or frost, drive only with winter-suitable tyres § 2 (3a) StVO . Since 1 October 2024 only tyres with the Alpine symbol (mountain with snowflake) count as winter-suitable. Pure M+S tyres are no longer enough. BOKraft also requires the equipment to suit road and weather conditions. If necessary: winter tyres, snow chains, tow rope § 18 BOKraft . Liability Anyone who has an accident on ice with summer tyres risks a fine and trouble with the comprehensive insurer (gross negligence). As the keeper you must not order or allow trips with unsuitable tyres either. Typical mistakes ! “Mietwagen don't need an alarm system.” § 25 BOKraft applies to taxis and Mietwagen. ! “A partition is mandatory in a taxi.” It is allowed but not mandatory. If fitted, it must be bullet-resistant. ! “Minimum tread 3 mm.” By law 1,6 mm; 3–4 mm are recommendations. ! “M+S is enough in winter.” Since October 2024 only tyres with the Alpine symbol. Summary Both 2 doors on the right, alarm system, licence number, advertising only on side doors; partition allowed Taxi only RAL 1015, taxi sign, taximeter, real-time navigation, 50 kg luggage, operator's address inside Mietwagen only distance meter; no taxi sign Every car first-aid kit, warning triangle, high-visibility vest Tyres min. 1,6 mm; winter: Alpine symbol in icy conditions"},{"t":"Lesson 3.4: Radio, standby and taxi ranks","u":"lektion-3-4-funk-bereitstellung.html","h":["Where may a taxi wait for customers?","The taxi rank","Behaviour at the rank (typical rules of local taxi regulations)","Radio, dispatch centre and app","Typical mistakes","Summary"],"x":"How does the passenger get to the taxi? At the taxi rank, by radio from the dispatch centre or by app. Waiting at the rank has clear rules from the PBefG, the StVO and the local taxi regulations. For your own radio you need a frequency from the Federal Network Agency. And Mietwagen have the duty to return when they are on standby. Where may a taxi wait for customers? Taxis may only be kept on standby at places approved by the authority § 47 (1) PBefG . In principle only in the municipality of the registered office . The authority can allow exceptions and define a larger area § 47 (2) PBefG . Passengers may stop a free taxi on the street by hand signal. The taxi may also accept bookings during a trip. Details of standby (order at the ranks, radio operation, service) are set by the state government or the municipality in the local taxi regulations ( Taxenordnung ) § 47 (3) PBefG . Mietwagen may not wait for customers on public roads. They wait at the registered office or at approved parking places ( lesson 1.1 ). The taxi rank Sign 229: taxi rank Taxenstand “Drivers must not stop at taxi ranks, except taxis that are kept ready for passenger transport.” Often with an additional sign showing how many taxis may stand there. StVO Annex 2, sign 229 This means: other cars may not even stop there briefly. A taxi may only stand there if it is ready for passengers, not to take a break or park. Behaviour at the rank (typical rules of local taxi regulations) Taxis line up in order of arrival and move up . The driver stays with the vehicle and is ready to drive at any time. The passenger has free choice : he may take any taxi at the rank, not only the first one. No washing, repairing or loud music at the rank. Be considerate of residents. If the rank is full, another taxi must not wait in the second row. The exact rules are in the taxi regulations of your city or district. Breaches are administrative offences. Radio, dispatch centre and app In the past most bookings came via the taxi radio of a dispatch centre. Today a lot runs via data radio and apps. For the exam you should know: Anyone who wants to run their own radio network (business radio) needs a frequency assignment from the Federal Network Agency ( Bundesnetzagentur ). The legal basis is the Telecommunications Act (TKG). The assignment costs fees. Business radio frequencies are usually shared by several users, for a certain area (e.g. a city). Radio devices must be approved (CE marking) and may only transmit on the assigned frequencies. Usually an operator joins a taxi dispatch centre ( Taxizentrale ) and pays a monthly radio fee (fixed costs, lesson 2.2 ). With app dispatch the operator usually pays a commission per trip (variable costs). Mietwagen and radio A booking for a Mietwagen by radio or app must also go via the registered office and be recorded there. Then the Mietwagen may accept a new booking during a trip (§ 49 (4) PBefG). Typical mistakes ! “I may stop briefly at the taxi rank to let someone out.” Not with a normal car. Only taxis on standby may stop there. ! “The passenger must take the first taxi in the line.” He has free choice. ! “The IHK assigns radio frequencies.” The Federal Network Agency does. Summary Standby only at approved places, in the municipality of the registered office (§ 47 PBefG) Sign 229 no stopping for anyone except taxis on standby At the rank order of arrival, move up, stay with the vehicle, free choice of the passenger; details in the Taxenordnung Radio frequency assignment by the Bundesnetzagentur (TKG) Mietwagen no standby on the street; bookings via the registered office"},{"t":"Lesson 4.1: Road safety and vehicle checks","u":"lektion-4-1-verkehrssicherheit.html","h":["The pre-departure check","Stopping distance: the rules of thumb","The biggest dangers in everyday taxi work","Instructing the drivers","Typical mistakes","Summary"],"x":"As an operator you are responsible for the safety of your vehicles and drivers. The exam expects you to be able to write instructions for your drivers: What does the driver check before the shift? How does he drive safely? What does he do when tired? This also includes a few rules of thumb for the stopping distance. The pre-departure check Under the accident prevention regulation “Vehicles” the driver must check before every shift whether the most important safety equipment works. He reports defects immediately. If a defect endangers safety, he must not drive off § 36 DGUV Vorschrift 70 . What exactly to check is described in DGUV Grundsatz 314-002. Area Check points Walk around outside tyres (pressure, tread, damage), lights and indicators, mirrors, windows clean and without cracks, number plates, taxi sign, licence number, accident damage Inside the vehicle brakes (pedal travel, parking brake), steering, horn, wipers and washers, warning lights on the display, seat belts, child seats, alarm system Equipment first-aid kit, warning triangle, high-visibility vest, taximeter/distance meter works and is calibrated, papers complete Fluids fuel or charge level, oil, coolant, washer fluid (regularly, not every shift) In addition, the operator has the vehicles checked for safe operating condition by a competent person at least once a year and records the result in writing § 57 DGUV Vorschrift 70 . For cars, according to the Berufsgenossenschaften, a defect-free service at a specialist workshop can be enough if it also covers occupational safety. Stopping distance: the rules of thumb Reaction distance (m) = (speed in km/h ÷ 10) × 3 Braking distance (m) = (speed in km/h ÷ 10)² Braking distance in an emergency stop (m) = (speed ÷ 10)² ÷ 2 Stopping distance = reaction distance + braking distance Speed km/h normal braking emergency stop Driving school rules of thumb for a dry road. On wet or icy roads or when tired the distance gets much longer. What does this mean? At 50 km/h a car needs about 40 m with normal braking, at 100 km/h already 130 m. Double the speed means four times the braking distance . In the city with many pedestrians every km/h less saves lives. The biggest dangers in everyday taxi work Danger Instruction to drivers Tiredness, microsleep Take breaks (ArbZG), stop immediately when tired. Coffee does not replace sleep. Plan and limit long night shifts. Distraction Don't hold the phone (§ 23 StVO). Set navigation and radio before driving. Take payment only when stationary. Too fast, too close Adapt speed to traffic and weather (§ 3 StVO); distance outside towns at least “half the speedometer” in metres (§ 4 StVO). Alcohol, drugs, medication Absolutely forbidden on duty (§ 8 BOKraft). With medication, read the package leaflet. Getting in and out Let passengers get out on the pavement side if possible, watch for cyclists (open doors slowly), avoid stopping in the second row with hazard lights: better look for a safe place to stop. Luggage Heavy luggage in the boot, not loose on the back seat or parcel shelf. In an emergency stop it becomes a projectile. Wheelchair transport Secure the wheelchair and the person with the prescribed restraint systems. Instructing the drivers The employer must instruct employees on safety and health ( Unterweisung ): when hiring, when something changes and after that at least once a year § 12 ArbSchG, § 4 DGUV Vorschrift 1 . The instruction is documented (date, content, drivers' signatures). Content: pre-departure check, safe driving, behaviour in accidents and robberies, first aid, dealing with difficult passengers, child seats, securing wheelchairs. The basis is the risk assessment ( Gefährdungsbeurteilung ): What dangers exist in my business, and what do I do about them? § 5 ArbSchG Sensible: driver safety training . Many Berufsgenossenschaften subsidise it. Typical mistakes ! “The garage does the pre-departure check once a year.” The driver does it before every shift. Additionally a competent person once a year. ! Braking distance at 100 km/h = 2 × braking distance at 50 km/h. Four times as long: (100 ÷ 10)² = 100 m instead of 25 m. ! “One instruction when hiring is enough.” Repeat at least yearly and document it. Summary Pre-departure check before every shift by the driver (§ 36 DGUV V 70) Competent person check at least yearly, in writing (§ 57 DGUV V 70) Formulas reaction distance (v/10)×3; braking distance (v/10)²; emergency stop ÷ 2 Instruction when hiring and at least yearly, documented (§ 12 ArbSchG)"},{"t":"Lesson 4.2: Preventing accidents and acting correctly","u":"lektion-4-2-unfaelle.html","h":["Behaviour at the scene of an accident","Reporting to the Berufsgenossenschaft","Preventing accidents: learn from every accident","Protection against robberies","Typical mistakes","Summary"],"x":"If you drive a lot, you will have an accident at some point. What matters is that driver and operator then act correctly: secure the scene, help, allow the facts to be established, report. Afterwards you ask yourself: how do I prevent the next accident? Robberies of taxi drivers are also part of accident prevention. Behaviour at the scene of an accident Stop immediately , hazard lights on § 34 (1) No. 1 StVO . Protect yourself : put on the high-visibility vest, let passengers get out on the safe side and take them behind the crash barrier. Secure the scene : set up the warning triangle (in town about 50 m, on country roads about 100 m, on the motorway at least 150–200 m before the scene) § 34 (1) No. 2 StVO . Emergency call 112 if someone is injured: Where? What happened? How many injured? What injuries? Wait for questions. Give first aid § 34 (1) No. 4 StVO . Anyone who does not help although it is reasonable commits a criminal offence § 323c StGB . Allow the facts to be established : give your name, address and insurance, show your driving licence and registration certificate on request § 34 (1) No. 5 StVO . Call the police if people are injured, the situation is unclear, there is an argument or alcohol is suspected. Document : photos, witnesses, accident report (a European accident report in the glove box is useful). Report : to the operator, the insurer (without delay, a contractual duty) and, if the driver is injured, possibly the Berufsgenossenschaft. Leaving the scene of an accident Anyone who leaves the scene after an accident without allowing the facts to be established or waiting a reasonable time commits leaving the scene of an accident ( Unfallflucht ). This is a criminal offence § 142 StGB . Consequences: fine or prison, often loss of the driving licence and with it the P-Schein, recourse by the insurer up to 5.000 €. This also applies to small dents, for example a parking bump. Reporting to the Berufsgenossenschaft A work accident is an accident at work or on the direct way to and from work (commuting accident). The operator must report it to the Berufsgenossenschaft if the insured person dies or is unable to work for more than 3 days . Deadline: within 3 days after the operator learns of it § 193 (1) and (4) SGB VII . An injured driver goes to a D-Arzt (accident insurance doctor) if he is unable to work for more than one day or needs further medical treatment. Report fatal accidents and accidents with many injured to the BG immediately , for example by phone. Preventing accidents: learn from every accident The exam expects you to be able to work out measures so that accidents and serious offences don't happen again. A four-step approach: Analyse : What happened, and why? Time, place, weather, driver, vehicle condition, shift length. Decide on measures , in this order: technical (e.g. emergency brake assist, winter tyres), organisational (shift plan, breaks, routes), personal (training, driver safety training). Instruct : inform all drivers about the case and the measures, document it. Check : Do the measures work? Keep accident statistics for the business. Basis: DGUV Vorschrift 1 “Principles of prevention” (risk assessment, instruction, first aiders) and DGUV Vorschrift 70 “Vehicles” (inspection, operation). Serious offences Repeated serious offences by your drivers (alcohol, leaving the scene, working time) can call your own reliability as an operator into question if you do nothing about them ( lesson 1.2 ). Protection against robberies Taxi drivers are robbed more often than many other professions. Measures: Technology Alarm system under § 25 BOKraft, possibly a partition, emergency button with location via the dispatch centre or app, camera (only in compliance with data protection, with a notice sign). Little cash Encourage card payment, remove cash regularly, keep only small change in the vehicle. Behaviour When threatened, don't resist, hand over the money. Your own safety comes first. Remember the offender, then call the police immediately. Aftercare A robbery is a work accident. Psychological first aid, D-Arzt, report to the BG. Typical mistakes ! “If I scratch a parked car, I leave a note and drive on.” That can be leaving the scene of an accident. Wait a reasonable time, otherwise call the police. ! “Accident report to the BG: within 4 weeks.” Within 3 days if unable to work for more than 3 days. ! “Put the warning triangle directly behind the car.” With distance: town about 50 m, country road about 100 m, motorway at least 150–200 m. Summary At the scene stop, protect yourself, secure, emergency call, first aid, allow facts to be established, document, report Criminal offences leaving the scene (§ 142 StGB), failure to render assistance (§ 323c StGB) BG report death or more than 3 days unable to work, within 3 days (§ 193 SGB VII) Prevention analyse → technical, organisational, personal → instruct → check Robbery alarm system, little cash, don't resist, report as a work accident"},{"t":"Lesson 4.3: Environmental protection","u":"lektion-4-3-umweltschutz.html","h":["Exhaust fumes and low-emission zones","Avoid noise and unnecessary exhaust fumes","Used oil, tyres, batteries, washing water","Driving economically and eco-friendly","Typical mistakes","Summary"],"x":"Taxis and Mietwagen drive a lot, mostly in town. That causes exhaust fumes and noise. As an operator you must know the basic rules of environmental protection: low-emission zones, unnecessary noise, correct handling of used oil, washing and waste. Protecting the environment often saves money too, above all on fuel. Exhaust fumes and low-emission zones Vehicles must meet the emission rules. This is checked with the emissions test during the general inspection § 47 StVZO . In low-emission zones ( Umweltzone , traffic sign 270.1) only vehicles with the right sticker ( Plakette ) may drive. The sticker shows the pollutant group 35. BImSchV . The basis for low-emission zones and clean air plans are the limits for air quality 39. BImSchV . The licensing authority can set requirements for taxis and Mietwagen on exhaust and emissions and make licences depend on them §§ 13 (5b), 64b PBefG . Aachen Aachen has a low-emission zone. Only vehicles with a green sticker may drive there (as of 2026). An exemption from the sticker duty has been decided for pure electric cars; check the current status with the City of Aachen. Avoid noise and unnecessary exhaust fumes Forbidden are § 30 (1) StVO : unnecessary noise and avoidable exhaust nuisance, letting engines run unnecessarily , for example at the taxi rank or while waiting for a passenger, slamming doors too loudly, pointless driving back and forth in built-up areas if this disturbs others. In practice at the taxi rank Engine off while waiting. Be quiet at night, radio low, close doors quietly. Otherwise residents complain to the authority, and that can cause trouble under the local taxi regulations. Used oil, tyres, batteries, washing water Substance Correct disposal Used oil Oil changes only in a workshop or at suitable places. Anyone who sells engine oil must take back the same amount of used oil free of charge. Never put used oil into the sewer, the ground or household waste KrWG, Altölverordnung . Washing water Don't wash vehicles on the street or on unpaved ground. Oil, fuel and cleaning agents would get into the groundwater. Car washes have an oil separator WHG . Old tyres Return them to the dealer or a disposal company, never dump them. Starter batteries Return them to the dealer (deposit). Not into household waste. Air-conditioning refrigerant Only a specialist workshop with certified staff. Driving economically and eco-friendly Fuel is one of the biggest variable cost items. Economical driving protects the environment and your wallet: Drive with foresight Take your foot off the gas early, let the car roll, brake little. Low engine speed Shift up early. With an automatic, accelerate gently. Tyre pressure Too little air increases consumption and wear. Check regularly. Ballast and roof racks Remove unnecessary load from the boot. Use air conditioning only when needed. Engine off When waiting, at the rank and during longer stops. Choice of vehicle Hybrid and electric vehicles reduce consumption, noise and exhaust in town. Electric cars are exempt from vehicle tax ( lesson 1.10 ). Calculation (example values) Mileage 70.000 km, consumption falls through training from 7,0 to 6,3 l/100 km Saving = 70.000 ÷ 100 × 0,7 l = 490 l 490 l × 1,55 €/l net = 759,50 € per year and vehicle Typical mistakes ! “In winter I leave the engine running at the rank for the heating.” Running the engine unnecessarily is forbidden (§ 30 StVO). ! “I quickly wash the taxi in front of my house.” In a car wash with an oil separator. ! “Used oil can go into general waste if it's in a canister.” Return it to the seller or a disposal company. Summary Exhaust emissions test at the HU (§ 47 StVZO); low-emission zones with sticker (35. BImSchV); Aachen: green sticker Noise no unnecessary noise, no idling, close doors quietly (§ 30 StVO) Waste used oil back to the dealer, wash only with an oil separator, return tyres and batteries Economical driving foresight, low engine speed, tyre pressure, engine off, hybrid/electric"},{"t":"Lesson 5.1: Trips abroad","u":"lektion-5-1-fahrten-ins-ausland.html","h":["The principle","What is cabotage?","Taxi and Mietwagen across the border","VAT","Typical mistakes","Summary"],"x":"From Aachen it is only a few minutes to the Netherlands and Belgium. Cross-border trips are everyday business for Aachen operators: to Maastricht airport, to Vaals, Kerkrade, Eupen or Liège. But what may a German taxi do abroad? Taking passengers there: yes. Driving around there: no. This lesson explains the basic rules. Exam area 5 is only tested where it matters for the IHK district, so in Aachen very likely. The principle For cross-border trips by motor vehicle, the PBefG applies on the German part of the route , whether the operator comes from Germany or abroad § 52 (1) PBefG . On the foreign part of the route the law of the other country applies. For taxis and Mietwagen there is no uniform EU law as there is for buses. Each country decides for itself what foreign taxis may do there. Foreign operators do not need a German licence for occasional transport to Germany if an agreement with their country covers this. Otherwise the Federal Ministry of Transport can allow an exception § 52 (3) PBefG . The border police and customs can turn back vehicles that do not carry a required licence § 52 (4) PBefG . Pure transit through Germany is governed by § 53 PBefG. What is cabotage? Cabotage ( Kabotage ) means: a foreign vehicle carries passengers within another country, i.e. from one place in the Netherlands to another place in the Netherlands. For taxis and Mietwagen, cabotage is forbidden in the neighbouring countries. Trip with an Aachen taxi Allowed? Aachen → Maastricht (passenger gets in in Aachen) yes Maastricht → Aachen, pre-booked yes under Dutch law only with a prior booking Maastricht → Aachen, passenger hails the taxi spontaneously in Maastricht no Vaals → Maastricht (both places in the Netherlands) no cabotage Aachen → Eupen (Belgium), then back empty yes Eupen → Liège (both in Belgium) no cabotage Aachen → through the Netherlands → Aachen (shortcut) yes transit The other way round, Dutch taxis may bring passengers to Germany, but may not carry them within Germany. In the Netherlands cabotage is punished with fines of about 2.000 € ; if the driver doesn't pay, the vehicle can be held. Check the current rules The rules of the neighbouring countries can change, and in Belgium the regions (Wallonia, Flanders, Brussels) are responsible. Before regular trips abroad, ask the licensing authority, the taxi association or the Euregio cross-border information service. Taxi and Mietwagen across the border Taxi tariff : the German tariff only applies within the mandatory service area ( Pflichtfahrbereich ). For trips abroad you agree the price before the trip starts , unless the local taxi regulations say otherwise. Duty to carry : only applies within the mandatory service area. A taxi may in principle refuse a trip abroad. Taxi sign : outside the tariff area it is not lit as “free”. You may not wait for customers abroad. Mietwagen : on trips abroad too, the booking must come via the registered office, and afterwards the car returns to the registered office. A Mietwagen may not wait for new customers abroad. VAT On cross-border trips only the part of the route in Germany is taxed in Germany. The reduced rate for taxis applies under the usual rules (municipality or up to 50 km). The part abroad falls under the tax law of the other country. If you drive abroad regularly, ask your tax adviser whether you need to register there. Typical mistakes ! “I can join the taxis in Vaals, Vaals is right next to Aachen.” Standby only in your own municipality in Germany. Abroad: cabotage. ! “My Aachen taxi tariff applies abroad.” Only in the mandatory service area. For trips abroad: agree the price beforehand. ! “The EU bus regulation applies to taxis.” There is no EU regulation for taxis; national law applies. Summary Law Germany: PBefG (§ 52); abroad: law of the other country; no EU rules for taxis Allowed taking passengers abroad; transit; NL: pre-booked pick-up to Germany Forbidden cabotage (trips within the other country), spontaneous pick-up abroad, waiting for customers abroad Price outside the mandatory service area, agree before the trip starts"},{"t":"Lesson 5.2: Passports and customs","u":"lektion-5-2-pass-zoll.html","h":["ID documents","Border checks","Goods across the border","Within the EU (e.g. NL, BE)","From non-EU countries (e.g. Switzerland)","Forbidden and restricted goods","Summary"],"x":"In the Schengen area there are normally no border checks. But since September 2024 Germany has been checking again at all land borders, including near Aachen. Drivers and passengers need valid ID. And anyone who takes goods across the border must know what is allowed. Especially important for taxi drivers in Aachen: cannabis from the Netherlands. ID documents Person Which document? Germans and other EU citizens valid ID card or passport Non-EU citizens living in Germany passport and residence permit Non-EU citizens as visitors passport , depending on the country with a visa (Schengen visa: usually up to 90 days in 180 days) Children their own ID card or passport (child passport) A driving licence is not an ID document . The driver needs his ID card or passport on every trip anyway (§ 2a SchwarzArbG). Border checks Germany, the Netherlands and Belgium are part of the Schengen area . Normally there are no checks at the borders. But since 16 September 2024 Germany has been checking temporarily at all land borders. The checks have been extended several times. So expect the Federal Police to check IDs at the border. Passenger without papers If a passenger has no valid ID or visa, there can be problems at a check. Anyone who knowingly takes people without a right of residence across the border and takes money for it can commit the offence of smuggling people (§ 96 AufenthG). If in doubt, don't drive. Goods across the border Within the EU (e.g. NL, BE) In the EU single market you may in principle take goods for your own use freely. For tobacco and alcohol there are guide quantities ( Richtmengen ). If you carry more, you must be able to show that it is for your own use: Cigarettes 800 pieces Cigarillos / cigars 400 / 200 pieces Smoking tobacco 1 kg Spirits 10 litres Intermediate products (e.g. sherry) 20 litres Wine (of which sparkling wine max. 60 l) 90 litres Beer 110 litres Cash of 10.000 € or more must be declared on request by customs when travelling within the EU. When entering or leaving the EU it must be declared in writing. From non-EU countries (e.g. Switzerland) Stricter duty-free allowances apply here, for example 200 cigarettes, 1 litre of spirits and other goods up to a value of 300 € (arrival by land). Above that, duties are due. This is rare for Aachen operators, but it can come up in the exam. Forbidden and restricted goods Goods Rule Narcotics (drugs) Import forbidden and a criminal offence (BtMG). Cannabis In Germany adults may possess small amounts since 2024. But importing it, even from a Dutch coffee shop, is forbidden (KCanG). Weapons and ammunition Only with a permit; within the EU with the European Firearms Pass (WaffG). Explosives, fireworks Forbidden or strongly restricted; foreign fireworks without German approval are forbidden. Counterfeit brand goods, protected animals and plants Forbidden or only with a permit. Medicines only for personal use (usually up to 3 months). As a taxi driver Dangerous substances (explosive, highly flammable) are excluded from transport anyway (§ 15 BOKraft). If you notice that a passenger wants to take forbidden goods across the border, you may refuse the trip. Never knowingly help with smuggling. Summary EU citizens ID card or passport Non-EU citizens passport + residence permit or visa Border checks since 16.9.2024 at German land borders, extended several times Goods in the EU own use free; guide quantities e.g. 800 cigarettes, 10 l spirits, 90 l wine, 110 l beer Forbidden drugs, importing cannabis, weapons without permit, explosives, counterfeits"},{"t":"Lesson 5.3: Documents for trips abroad","u":"lektion-5-3-dokumente-ausland.html","h":["Checklist for trips abroad","Equipment in neighbouring countries","Summary"],"x":"Which papers belong in the vehicle when you cross the border? In principle the same as at home, plus ID and a few useful extras. This short lesson sums it all up. Checklist for trips abroad Relates to Document Note Driver ID card or passport required at border checks; a driving licence is not an ID Driving licence EU driving licences are valid throughout the EU P-Schein required for transport under German law; abroad the other country's rules also apply Vehicle Registration certificate part I paper or i-Kfz app; abroad paper is safer, because the app is not known everywhere Proof of motor liability insurance (international motor insurance card, “Green Card”) Within the EU the number plate counts as proof of insurance; the card is still helpful after accidents and mandatory in some non-EU countries European accident report a standard form in all languages that makes reporting damage easier Company Licence or shortened official copy § 17 (4) PBefG; border police and customs can ask for it (§ 52 (4)) for Mietwagen: proof of booking shows that the booking came via the registered office (important to avoid suspicion of cabotage) Passengers Passengers also need valid ID documents ( lesson 5.2 ). Point this out when they book, for example for airport trips to Maastricht or Liège. Equipment in neighbouring countries Some countries have different equipment rules. In Belgium and the Netherlands the German mandatory equipment (warning triangle, first-aid kit, high-visibility vest) is usually enough. For trips further away, for example to France or Luxembourg, find out beforehand, for instance from the ADAC. Summary Driver ID card/passport, driving licence, P-Schein Vehicle registration part I, Green Card (recommended), European accident report Company licence/copy; Mietwagen: proof of booking"},{"t":"Lesson 6.1: The exam: format, registration and strategy","u":"lektion-6-1-pruefung-ablauf.html","h":["Structure and points","Pass rules","Registration and costs","What to bring?","Tactics on exam day","Manage your time","Open questions","Calculations","The oral exam","A 6-week study plan","Summary"],"x":"Finally, the exam itself: where do you register, what does it cost, how is it structured, and how do you get as many points as possible on exam day? With a clear study plan and the right tactics the exam is very manageable. Structure and points Part Content Time (min.) Weight Points Written part 1 Multiple-choice questions and questions with a direct answer 60 min. 40 % 60 Written part 2 Exercises and case studies, e.g. cost accounting 60 min. 35 % 52,5 Oral Conversation with the examination board, only if needed up to 30 min. 25 % 37,5 § 4 PBZugV (weighting, passing); points and times according to the IHKs Pass rules At least 50 % in each written part (part 1: 30 points, part 2: 26,25 points). Otherwise you fail, without an oral exam. Written parts together at least 90 points (60 % of 150)? Then you pass without the oral exam. Both parts above 50 % but together below 90 points? Then there is an oral exam . You pass if you reach at least 90 of 150 points in total. Example Part 1: 42 of 60 (70 %) ✓ above 50 % Part 2: 33 of 52,5 (63 %) ✓ above 50 % Written: 75 points → below 90 → oral exam Needed in the oral: 90 − 75 = 15 of 37,5 points (40 %) Registration and costs The responsible body is the IHK where you live § 5 (4) PBZugV . The IHK offers dates at least every quarter § 5 (4) PBZugV . Many dates are booked up quickly. Register early; some IHKs recommend several months in advance. The fee is usually between about 190 and 280 €, depending on the IHK. You pay it again for every attempt. More and more IHKs hold the exam on a computer . Ask your IHK how it works for you, and practise on screen too, for example with the mock exam here. If you pass, you receive a certificate of professional competence. It is valid indefinitely and is required for the licence. IHK Aachen (as of autumn 2026) Exam fee: 259 € (since 26.11.2025). For 2026, written exams were scheduled in September and November and oral exams in October and December. Registration opens at fixed times, and some dates were booked up immediately. Check the current dates on the IHK Aachen website and register as soon as registration opens. What to bring? a valid ID card or passport the invitation from the IHK a calculator if allowed (usually non-programmable, without internet). The invitation says exactly what is allowed. pens, a drink, reading glasses if needed The exam is in German The IHK exam is held in German. Learn the German technical terms: they are shown in this English version in italics and in the glossary . You can also switch any page to German with the language button. Tactics on exam day Manage your time In part 1 you have about 2 minutes per question . Don't get stuck on one question. Mark it and come back at the end. 1. Overview Skim all questions. Answer the ones you are sure about first. 2. Read carefully Watch for nicht (not), immer (always), nur (only), mindestens (at least). In multiple choice: how many answers are correct? 3. Leave nothing blank In multiple choice, guessing is better than nothing. In open questions every correct keyword earns points. 4. Remaining time The last 5 minutes: marked questions, check calculations. Open questions Write in bullet points . Full sentences cost time and earn no extra points. Use the technical terms ( Rückkehrpflicht, Beförderungspflicht, Abschreibung …) and, if you know it, the section of the law. If the question asks for “four differences”, name four, not two and not ten. Calculations Write down every step with formula and unit. Even with a calculation error you get points for the correct method. Follow the fixed scheme: depreciation → interest → fixed costs → variable costs per km → total costs → per km → per occupied km ( lesson 2.2 ). At the end, check whether the result is realistic: costs per km for a taxi are usually somewhere around 1 €, not 10 € or 0,01 €. Net or gross? If the task doesn't say, calculate net and write that down. The oral exam The oral exam is a conversation, often about topics where the written part didn't go well. Tips: Answer briefly and honestly. If you don't know something, say so and offer what you do know about it. Use practical examples: “If my driver …, then …”. Practise out loud with the open questions here (the mock exam has a simulation for this). A 6-week study plan Week Learn Practise 1 Module 1, lessons 1.1 to 1.5 Knowledge check of each lesson, flashcards 10 minutes a day 2 Module 1, lessons 1.6 to 1.10 Question trainer module 1 3 Module 2, above all cost accounting and pricing Calculation trainer: one complete task every day 4 Modules 3, 4 and 5 Mixed question trainer, repeat mistakes 5 Weak spots from “My progress” 2 mock exams under time pressure 6 Revise, read the summaries 1 mock exam, flashcards, rest before the exam Goal before the exam If you reliably score more than 90 points in the mock exams here and do the cost accounting in the calculation trainer without mistakes, you are well prepared. Summary Structure part 1: 60 pts, part 2: 52,5 pts, oral: 37,5 pts; total 150 Passing each written part ≥ 50 %; total ≥ 90 points (60 %) Oral only if both written parts ≥ 50 % but together Registration IHK where you live, register early; Aachen: 259 € Tactics 2 min. per question, bullet points, calculation steps with units, leave nothing blank Take a mock exam now"},{"t":"Glossary: Nachweisgesetz (Act on written statement of employment conditions)","u":"glossar.html#nachwg","h":[],"x":"Law under which the employer must record the essential working conditions in writing and give them to the employee. In the taxi trade on paper with a signature."},{"t":"Glossary: Mindestlohn (statutory minimum wage)","u":"glossar.html#mindestlohn","h":[],"x":"Statutory wage that every employee must at least receive per hour: 13,90 € (2026), 14,60 € (2027)."},{"t":"Glossary: Minijob (mini-job (marginal employment))","u":"glossar.html#minijob","h":[],"x":"Marginal employment with earnings up to the mini-job limit (2026: 603 € per month)."},{"t":"Glossary: Entgeltfortzahlung (continued pay during sickness)","u":"glossar.html#entgeltfortzahlung","h":[],"x":"The employer keeps paying wages during sickness for up to 6 weeks (EFZG)."},{"t":"Glossary: Kündigungsfrist (notice period)","u":"glossar.html#kuendigungsfrist","h":[],"x":"Time between notice and the end of employment. Basic period: 4 weeks to the 15th or the end of a month (§ 622 BGB)."},{"t":"Glossary: Abmahnung (formal warning)","u":"glossar.html#abmahnung","h":[],"x":"Written warning by the employer about misconduct. Usually needed before dismissal for conduct."},{"t":"Glossary: Genehmigung (licence, permit)","u":"glossar.html#genehmigung","h":[],"x":"Permission from the authority to carry people for money. Without it the trip is forbidden. Valid for at most 5 years."},{"t":"Glossary: Gelegenheitsverkehr (occasional (non-scheduled) passenger transport)","u":"glossar.html#gelegenheitsverkehr","h":[],"x":"Passenger transport without a fixed timetable or route. Only four forms are allowed, including taxi and Mietwagen (§ 46 PBefG)."},{"t":"Glossary: Betriebssitz (registered place of business)","u":"glossar.html#betriebssitz","h":[],"x":"The place where the company is based. For a Mietwagen all bookings must arrive there, and it must return there after the trip."},{"t":"Glossary: Rückkehrpflicht (duty to return (to base))","u":"glossar.html#rueckkehrpflicht","h":[],"x":"A Mietwagen must return to the registered office without delay after each trip, unless it received a new booking via the registered office before or during the trip (§ 49 (4) PBefG)."},{"t":"Glossary: Beförderungspflicht (obligation to carry passengers)","u":"glossar.html#befoerderungspflicht","h":[],"x":"The taxi operator's duty to take passengers. Applies only within the mandatory service area and not to Mietwagen (§ 22, § 47 (4) PBefG)."},{"t":"Glossary: Pflichtfahrbereich (mandatory service area)","u":"glossar.html#pflichtfahrbereich","h":[],"x":"The area where the taxi tariff applies. Only here does the duty to carry apply. Usually the municipality or district."},{"t":"Glossary: Betriebspflicht (obligation to operate)","u":"glossar.html#betriebspflicht","h":[],"x":"Duty to actually run and maintain the licensed service (§ 21 PBefG). Applies to taxis, not to Mietwagen."},{"t":"Glossary: Taxitarif (taxi fare (official rate))","u":"glossar.html#taxitarif","h":[],"x":"Binding prices for taxi trips, set by statutory regulation (§ 51 PBefG). Usually made up of a base fare, a price per km and waiting time."},{"t":"Glossary: Mindestbeförderungsentgelt (minimum fare)","u":"glossar.html#mindestentgelt","h":[],"x":"Minimum price for a Mietwagen trip. The authority can set it but doesn't have to (§ 51a PBefG)."},{"t":"Glossary: Taxischild (taxi roof sign)","u":"glossar.html#taxischild","h":[],"x":"Illuminated roof sign saying “Taxi”. Mandatory for taxis, forbidden for Mietwagen (§ 26 BOKraft)."},{"t":"Glossary: Fahrpreisanzeiger (taximeter)","u":"glossar.html#fahrpreisanzeiger","h":[],"x":"The taximeter. A calibrated device that shows the fare. Mandatory in a taxi (§ 28 BOKraft)."},{"t":"Glossary: Wegstreckenzähler (odometer (calibrated))","u":"glossar.html#wegstreckenzaehler","h":[],"x":"Calibrated device that measures the distance driven. Mandatory in a Mietwagen (§ 30 BOKraft)."},{"t":"Glossary: Abstellort (approved parking location)","u":"glossar.html#abstellort","h":[],"x":"Place approved by the authority where a Mietwagen in large rural municipalities may wait instead of at the registered office (§ 49 (5) PBefG)."},{"t":"Glossary: Fixkosten (fixed costs)","u":"glossar.html#fixkosten","h":[],"x":"Costs that arise even when the car is standing still. For example insurance, vehicle tax, time-based depreciation, owner's salary."},{"t":"Glossary: variable Kosten (variable costs)","u":"glossar.html#variable-kosten","h":[],"x":"Costs that rise with every kilometre driven or with turnover. For example fuel, tyres, maintenance, commission."},{"t":"Glossary: Abschreibung (depreciation)","u":"glossar.html#abschreibung","h":[],"x":"The loss of value of a vehicle, spread over the years of use. Formula: (purchase price − residual value) ÷ useful life."},{"t":"Glossary: kalkulatorische Zinsen (imputed interest)","u":"glossar.html#kalk-zinsen","h":[],"x":"Interest on the money tied up in the vehicle. It is counted even if you have no loan. Formula: (purchase price + residual value) ÷ 2 × interest rate."},{"t":"Glossary: kalkulatorischer Unternehmerlohn (imputed owner's salary)","u":"glossar.html#unternehmerlohn","h":[],"x":"Your own salary as the owner. It is included as a cost so that the price also pays for your work."},{"t":"Glossary: Besetztkilometer (occupied (paid) kilometres)","u":"glossar.html#besetztkilometer","h":[],"x":"Kilometres driven with a passenger. Only they bring turnover. Empty kilometres must be paid for too."},{"t":"Glossary: Provision (commission)","u":"glossar.html#provision","h":[],"x":"Share of turnover that a booking platform (e.g. an app) keeps. It is deducted from turnover."},{"t":"Glossary: Deckungsbeitrag (contribution margin)","u":"glossar.html#deckungsbeitrag","h":[],"x":"Turnover minus variable costs. This amount covers the fixed costs. What is left after that is profit."},{"t":"Glossary: Restwert (residual value)","u":"glossar.html#restwert","h":[],"x":"What the vehicle is still worth at the end of its useful life, for example when sold."},{"t":"Glossary: Nutzungsdauer (useful life)","u":"glossar.html#nutzungsdauer","h":[],"x":"How many years you plan to use the vehicle in the business. Basis for depreciation."},{"t":"Glossary: Gewinnschwelle (break-even point)","u":"glossar.html#break-even","h":[],"x":"The point where turnover and total costs are equal. From here on the business makes a profit."},{"t":"Glossary: Kassenbuch (cash book)","u":"glossar.html#kassenbuch","h":[],"x":"Daily record of all cash receipts and payments with the cash balance. The balance must never be negative."},{"t":"Glossary: Einnahmen-Überschuss-Rechnung (EÜR) (cash-basis profit calculation)","u":"glossar.html#euer","h":[],"x":"Simple profit calculation: business income minus business expenses, by date of payment (§ 4 (3) EStG)."},{"t":"Glossary: Schichtzettel (shift sheet)","u":"glossar.html#schichtzettel","h":[],"x":"Record per shift: driver, vehicle, mileage readings, occupied km, trips, takings. Basic record for the tax office."},{"t":"Glossary: AfA (Absetzung für Abnutzung) (tax depreciation)","u":"glossar.html#afa","h":[],"x":"Depreciation for tax purposes. According to the industry depreciation table a taxi is written off over 5 years, without residual value."},{"t":"Glossary: Bilanz (balance sheet)","u":"glossar.html#bilanz","h":[],"x":"Comparison of assets and capital on a reporting date. Both sides are equal."},{"t":"Glossary: GoBD (German principles of proper digital bookkeeping)","u":"glossar.html#gobd","h":[],"x":"Principles for the proper keeping and storage of books in electronic form. Complete, correct, timely, unalterable."},{"t":"Glossary: Fahrerlaubnis zur Fahrgastbeförderung (P-Schein) (passenger transport driving permit)","u":"glossar.html#p-schein","h":[],"x":"Additional permit for drivers of taxis, Mietwagen and other vehicles under the PBefG. From age 21, valid for at most 5 years (§ 48 FeV)."},{"t":"Glossary: Kinderrückhalteeinrichtung (child restraint system (child seat))","u":"glossar.html#kinderrueckhalteeinrichtung","h":[],"x":"Child seat or booster seat. Mandatory for children under 12 and under 150 cm. Relief in taxis: 2 children from 9 kg, 1 of them 9–18 kg (§ 21 StVO)."},{"t":"Glossary: Fahreignungsregister (driver fitness register (points register))","u":"glossar.html#fahreignungsregister","h":[],"x":"Register at the Federal Motor Transport Authority in Flensburg with points for traffic offences. An extract is needed for the P-Schein."},{"t":"Glossary: Führungszeugnis (certificate of conduct (criminal record extract))","u":"glossar.html#fuehrungszeugnis","h":[],"x":"Extract from the Federal Central Register about criminal records. Needed for the P-Schein and often for the licence."},{"t":"Glossary: Zuverlässigkeit (reliability, good repute)","u":"glossar.html#zuverlaessigkeit","h":[],"x":"Requirement for the licence: there are no signs that the operator breaks the law, e.g. serious crimes or offences against working time, tax or road safety rules (§ 1 PBZugV)."},{"t":"Glossary: finanzielle Leistungsfähigkeit (financial standing)","u":"glossar.html#finanzielle-leistungsfaehigkeit","h":[],"x":"Enough money to run the business. For taxi and Mietwagen at least 2.250 € equity for the first and 1.250 € for each further vehicle (§ 2 PBZugV)."},{"t":"Glossary: fachliche Eignung (professional competence)","u":"glossar.html#fachliche-eignung","h":[],"x":"The knowledge needed to run a transport business. Usually proven by the professional competence exam at the IHK (§ 13 PBefG, §§ 3–7 PBZugV)."},{"t":"Glossary: Eigenkapitalbescheinigung (certificate of equity capital)","u":"glossar.html#eigenkapitalbescheinigung","h":[],"x":"Confirmation of equity from a tax adviser, auditor or bank. May be at most 1 year old when you apply."},{"t":"Glossary: Unbedenklichkeitsbescheinigung (certificate of good standing (no arrears))","u":"glossar.html#unbedenklichkeitsbescheinigung","h":[],"x":"Confirmation from the tax office, municipality, health insurer or Berufsgenossenschaft that there are no arrears. May be at most 3 months old."},{"t":"Glossary: Genehmigungsurkunde (licence document)","u":"glossar.html#genehmigungsurkunde","h":[],"x":"The licence document. Contains among other things the operator, type of transport, period of validity and the vehicles' number plates. In the vehicle: the document or a shortened official copy (§ 17 PBefG)."},{"t":"Glossary: Genehmigungsbehörde (licensing authority)","u":"glossar.html#genehmigungsbehoerde","h":[],"x":"The authority that grants the licence and supervises the business. Usually the city or district administration (§ 54 PBefG)."},{"t":"Glossary: Widerruf (revocation)","u":"glossar.html#widerruf","h":[],"x":"The authority withdraws the licence, e.g. if the operator is no longer reliable (§ 25 PBefG)."},{"t":"Glossary: Kabotage (cabotage)","u":"glossar.html#kabotage","h":[],"x":"A foreign vehicle carries passengers within another country, e.g. an Aachen taxi from Vaals to Maastricht. Forbidden for taxis."},{"t":"Glossary: Schengen-Raum (Schengen area)","u":"glossar.html#schengen","h":[],"x":"Countries between which there are normally no border checks. Germany has temporarily been checking at its land borders again since 2024."},{"t":"Glossary: Richtmenge (guide level (excise goods))","u":"glossar.html#richtmenge","h":[],"x":"Amount of tobacco or alcohol up to which, within the EU, own use is assumed without proof, e.g. 800 cigarettes."},{"t":"Glossary: Grüne Karte (green card (international insurance))","u":"glossar.html#gruene-karte","h":[],"x":"International motor insurance card as proof of motor liability insurance abroad."},{"t":"Glossary: Sofortmeldung (immediate registration (of new employees))","u":"glossar.html#sofortmeldung","h":[],"x":"Report of a new employee to the pension insurance at the latest when work starts. Mandatory in passenger transport (§ 28a (4) SGB IV)."},{"t":"Glossary: Berufsgenossenschaft (statutory accident insurance institution)","u":"glossar.html#berufsgenossenschaft","h":[],"x":"Provider of statutory accident insurance. For taxi and Mietwagen: BG Verkehr. Only the employer pays contributions."},{"t":"Glossary: Einzugsstelle (collecting agency (health insurer))","u":"glossar.html#einzugsstelle","h":[],"x":"The health insurer that collects and distributes the total social insurance contribution. For mini-jobs the Minijob-Zentrale."},{"t":"Glossary: Scheinselbständigkeit (bogus self-employment)","u":"glossar.html#scheinselbstaendigkeit","h":[],"x":"Someone is officially self-employed but works like an employee. Consequence: the client must pay all social contributions retrospectively."},{"t":"Glossary: Beitragsbemessungsgrenze (contribution ceiling)","u":"glossar.html#beitragsbemessungsgrenze","h":[],"x":"Social contributions are calculated on income up to this amount. Not above it."},{"t":"Glossary: Umsatzsteuer (value added tax (VAT))","u":"glossar.html#umsatzsteuer","h":[],"x":"Tax on services, commonly called VAT. Taxi 7 % (municipality or ≤ 50 km), otherwise 19 %."},{"t":"Glossary: Vorsteuer (input VAT)","u":"glossar.html#vorsteuer","h":[],"x":"VAT that you pay when buying. It is deducted from your own VAT (§ 15 UStG)."},{"t":"Glossary: Zahllast (VAT payable)","u":"glossar.html#zahllast","h":[],"x":"VAT minus input tax. You pay this amount to the tax office."},{"t":"Glossary: Kleinunternehmer (small business (VAT exemption))","u":"glossar.html#kleinunternehmer","h":[],"x":"Small business owner with low turnover (previous year ≤ 25.000 €, current year ≤ 100.000 €). Charges no VAT and may not deduct input tax (§ 19 UStG)."},{"t":"Glossary: Kleinbetragsrechnung (simplified invoice (small amount))","u":"glossar.html#kleinbetragsrechnung","h":[],"x":"Simplified invoice up to 250 € gross, e.g. the taxi receipt. Name/address, date, service, gross amount, tax rate (§ 33 UStDV)."},{"t":"Glossary: Technische Sicherheitseinrichtung (TSE) (technical security device (fiscal module))","u":"glossar.html#tse","h":[],"x":"Certified module that protects records in cash registers and taximeters against manipulation. Mandatory in taxis since 1.1.2026."},{"t":"Glossary: Hebesatz (municipal multiplier (trade tax))","u":"glossar.html#hebesatz","h":[],"x":"Percentage that each municipality sets for trade tax. Tax base amount × multiplier = trade tax."},{"t":"Glossary: Tarifkorridor (fare corridor (price range))","u":"glossar.html#tarifkorridor","h":[],"x":"Price range with a minimum and maximum price for pre-booked taxi trips. The exact price is agreed before the trip starts (§ 51 (1) sentence 4 PBefG)."},{"t":"Glossary: Tarifpflicht (obligation to charge the official fare)","u":"glossar.html#tarifpflicht","h":[],"x":"The taxi tariff is binding. You may not charge more or less (§ 39 (3), § 51 (5) PBefG)."},{"t":"Glossary: Sondervereinbarung (special (contract) agreement)","u":"glossar.html#sondervereinbarung","h":[],"x":"Different price agreed with a major customer within the mandatory service area. Only allowed under four conditions, including written form (§ 51 (2) PBefG)."},{"t":"Glossary: Taxenordnung (local taxi regulations)","u":"glossar.html#taxenordnung","h":[],"x":"Local regulation with rules for taxis, e.g. behaviour at the taxi rank, service hours, mandatory service area. Often together with the taxi tariff."},{"t":"Glossary: Grundpreis (base fare, flag fall)","u":"glossar.html#grundpreis","h":[],"x":"Fixed amount charged first on every taxi trip."},{"t":"Glossary: Gewerbeanzeige (business registration)","u":"glossar.html#gewerbeanzeige","h":[],"x":"Registration of a trade with the municipal trade office (§ 14 GewO). Taxi and Mietwagen businesses must do this too."},{"t":"Glossary: Kaufmann (merchant (under commercial law))","u":"glossar.html#kaufmann","h":[],"x":"Anyone who runs a commercial business (§ 1 HGB), i.e. a business that needs commercial organisation. A GmbH and UG are always merchants."},{"t":"Glossary: Handelsregister (commercial register)","u":"glossar.html#handelsregister","h":[],"x":"Public register of merchants and companies at the local court. Mandatory for merchants, OHG, KG, GmbH, UG."},{"t":"Glossary: Haftung (liability)","u":"glossar.html#haftung","h":[],"x":"Being responsible for debts or damage. Unlimited = also with private assets. Limited = only with the contribution or the company's assets."},{"t":"Glossary: Stammkapital (share capital)","u":"glossar.html#stammkapital","h":[],"x":"Share capital of a GmbH, at least 25.000 €. At least 12.500 € must be paid in at registration."},{"t":"Glossary: Kommanditist (limited partner)","u":"glossar.html#kommanditist","h":[],"x":"Partner in a KG who is only liable up to the amount of his contribution. The general partner (Komplementär), by contrast, is fully liable."},{"t":"Glossary: Kfz-Haftpflichtversicherung (motor third-party liability insurance)","u":"glossar.html#kfz-haftpflicht","h":[],"x":"Mandatory insurance for every vehicle. Pays for damage you cause to others. At least 7,5 m € personal injury, 1,3 m € property damage."},{"t":"Glossary: Kaskoversicherung (comprehensive / collision insurance)","u":"glossar.html#kasko","h":[],"x":"Insurance for damage to your own vehicle. Partial cover: theft, fire, glass, animals. Comprehensive: also own-fault accidents."},{"t":"Glossary: Obliegenheit (contractual duty of the insured)","u":"glossar.html#obliegenheit","h":[],"x":"Duty of conduct under the insurance contract, e.g. no driver without a licence, report damage. Breaking it risks recourse."},{"t":"Glossary: Regress (recourse claim)","u":"glossar.html#regress","h":[],"x":"The insurer reclaims money it paid from the insured person, e.g. after drunk driving. In motor liability usually up to 5.000 €."},{"t":"Glossary: Beförderungsvertrag (contract of carriage)","u":"glossar.html#befoerderungsvertrag","h":[],"x":"Contract for a trip between passenger and operator. A contract for work (§ 631 BGB). It also arises without words, e.g. by getting in and naming the destination."},{"t":"Glossary: Halter (registered keeper)","u":"glossar.html#halter","h":[],"x":"The person who uses the vehicle for their own account and decides about it, usually the operator. Liable under § 7 StVG even without fault."},{"t":"Glossary: Gefährdungshaftung (strict liability)","u":"glossar.html#gefaehrdungshaftung","h":[],"x":"Liability without fault, because a thing (e.g. a car) creates a special danger. Example: keeper's liability under § 7 StVG."},{"t":"Glossary: Werkvertrag (contract for work (result owed))","u":"glossar.html#werkvertrag","h":[],"x":"Contract in which a specific result is owed, e.g. a trip to the destination or a repair (§ 631 BGB)."},{"t":"Glossary: Verjährung (limitation period)","u":"glossar.html#verjaehrung","h":[],"x":"After a deadline a claim can no longer be enforced. Rule: 3 years from the end of the year in which it arose."},{"t":"Glossary: Leasing (leasing)","u":"glossar.html#leasing","h":[],"x":"Renting a vehicle over several years for monthly payments. The leasing company remains the owner."},{"t":"Glossary: Skonto (cash discount (early payment))","u":"glossar.html#skonto","h":[],"x":"Discount for quick payment, e.g. 2 % for payment within 10 days. Calculated per year often more than 30 % interest."},{"t":"Glossary: Sicherungsübereignung (transfer of ownership as security)","u":"glossar.html#sicherungsuebereignung","h":[],"x":"The bank becomes owner of the vehicle as security, the operator keeps using it. The bank keeps the vehicle title."},{"t":"Glossary: Liquidität (liquidity)","u":"glossar.html#liquiditaet","h":[],"x":"The ability to pay bills on time. Ratio: liquid funds (and receivables) compared to short-term debts."},{"t":"Glossary: Eigenkapitalquote (equity ratio)","u":"glossar.html#eigenkapitalquote","h":[],"x":"Share of equity in total capital in percent. The higher, the more independent of banks."},{"t":"Glossary: SEPA-Lastschrift (SEPA direct debit)","u":"glossar.html#lastschrift","h":[],"x":"The payee collects money from the payer's account. This needs a mandate. SEPA core direct debit: 8 weeks right of return."}];
